[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130390 :tutorial/content "We can express $\\rho,$ $\\theta,$ and $\\phi$ in terms of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ using the following relations:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}, \\qquad\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad\n\\cos\\phi = \\dfrac{z}{\\rho}.\n$$\n\nTo help to understand where these formulas come from, note that:\n\n- The formula for $\\rho$ is given by the distance of the point $(x,y,z)$ from the origin.\n\n- Since the angle $\\theta$ corresponds to the same polar angle that we see in plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the relationship between $x,y,$ and $\\theta$ in spherical polar coordinates is the same as for plane polar coordinates.\n\n- The formula for $\\phi$ is simply a rearrangement of $z = \\rho\\cos\\phi.$\n\nLet's again consider our point $P$ with Cartesian coordinates $(1,\\sqrt{3}, 2),$ and convert the coordinates into spherical polar coordinates.\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since  $(x,y) = (1,\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the first quadrant, we can compute the corresponding angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{arctan}|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $\\phi$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{z}{\\rho }) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $P$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt2, \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{\\pi}4 \\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b21b7ae-3cd3-5a7f-a35c-aca967d9f5d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4117 :tutorial/title "Converting From Cartesian Coordinates to Spherical Polar Coordinates"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f849fe7f-670f-5a7f-8dc7-dbbd33a876f1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7707 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135056 :tutorial/content "Consider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe already know how to find an orthogonal basis for $2$-dimensional subspaces. But, how can we do it for subspaces with higher dimensions, like $V?$\n\nGood news is that we can extend the **Gram-Schmidt process**. Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, let $S=\\textrm{Span}(\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2).$ We find ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}\\,{a}_{3}\\,-\\,{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{2}}\\,{a}_{3} \\\\  & ={a}_{3}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}-\\frac{{a}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}}{v}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/5b/5b52c26738f2b4dd577ab4148ef7673ad4a1aa53c82a6a6c1e7bf46c0ff36df0.png)\n\nNotice that $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3$ is written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}),$ $\\mathbf{a}_2(={\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}),$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3.$ This means that ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\in V,$ and also ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\perp S.$ Moreover, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3\\} = V = \\textrm{Span}\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} \\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-(\\frac{2}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-(-\\frac{3}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "066a02aa-4c3e-5e75-8147-7e723bac3b9e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3633 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135132 :tutorial/content "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f13baac9-fbd8-5a28-b168-f3010a604b1b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5840 :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135177 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a square $n\\times n$ matrix $Q$ with columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{q}_n{:}$\n\n![](images/1b/1b69d138277cdd7ee291aaa70b81849dee28fa7112b821beaaab2235cde6c17b.svg)\n\nThe matrix $Q$ is said to be **orthogonal** if its columns $\\mathbf{q}_1, \\mathbf{q}_2, \\dots, \\mathbf{q}_n$ form an *orthonormal* set. In other words, \n\n- the columns of $Q$ must be *mutually orthogonal*, and\n\n- they must all be *unit* vectors.\n\nFor instance, the matrix \n![](images/8c/8c5cdeffc5e91988c140e53623b252c100f86e573a4f2c5a761b5e7667cce9ef.svg) \nis orthogonal since its columns \n\n![](images/87/874cd61d37f79fc732f36b7b3a2578b4957dde1a364522674cb3c93fe4c50ae8.svg)\n\nform an orthonormal set $\\left\\{ {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_1}, {\\color{black}\\mathbf{q}_2} \\right\\}.$ To see this, note the following:\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are orthogonal, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Both $\\mathbf q_1$ and $\\mathbf q_2$ are unit vectors, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{q}_{1}|{|}^{2} & ={q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{q}_{2}|{|}^{2} & ={q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6e985a62-c62b-5e6d-8c21-8c92fc9d0eca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8095 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135185 :tutorial/content "A number is *not* a surd if we can write it as a positive or negative whole number or fraction. \n\nFor example, $\\sqrt 9$ is *not* a surd because it is a perfect square and can be expressed as a whole number:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 9 = \\sqrt{3^2} = 3\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{16}}$ is *not* a surd because it can be written as a regular fraction (i.e., as the ratio of two whole numbers):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1\\cdot 1}{4\\cdot 4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we cannot have surds involving square roots of negative numbers. In other words, the numbers \n\n$$\n\\sqrt{-2}, \\qquad \\sqrt{-5}, \\qquad \\sqrt{-14}\n$$\n\nare *not* surds because they are *not real numbers*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "1d377452-f2b4-5c72-80ce-d643f8f571be" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8363 :tutorial/title "Numbers That Are Not Surds"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139661 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to simplify the following expression: \n\n$$\n2+\\dfrac{5}{2}x -5 + \\dfrac{7}{2}x.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWe begin by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{2}x+\\frac{7}{2}x)+(2-5) &= (\\frac{5+7}{2})x+(-3) \\\\ &= \\frac{12}{2}x-3 \\\\ &= 6x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the simplified expression is $6x-3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7ef8a0d2-a9fe-57f8-b3fc-7b69cbb3a818" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8040 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139713 :tutorial/content "We need to be extra careful when converting from one unit of area to another.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a square has an area of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$. Let's use unit arguments to express this area in square millimeters.\n\nWe start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from $\\textrm{cm}^2$ to $\\textrm{mm}^2$, we start with the conversion between $\\textrm{cm}$ and $\\textrm{mm}$. According to our table, there are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\n**Watch Out!** This is *not* the conversion factor we need. If we multiply $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by this conversion factor, we will *not* get an equivalent area!\n\nThe problem with using this conversion factor is that it does not contain area units. However, we can convert this into area units by squaring it and applying the rules of exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{10mm}{cm})^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is now the correct conversion factor. It tells us that there are $100$ square millimeters per square centimeter.\n\nNow, multiplying $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 2 \\cdot  100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $2\\,\\textrm{cm}^2$ is equivalent to $200\\,\\textrm{mm}^2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "418a46ad-4b9c-5bd6-961d-46c456cb6fb1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8744 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148664 :tutorial/content "We will find the Laplace transform of $\\cos(t)$ by rewriting it using complex exponentials and applying linearity.\n\nFirst, we recall the identity\n\n$$\n\\cos(t) = \\dfrac{e^{\\mathrm{i} t} + e^{-\\mathrm{i} t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we take the Laplace transform of *both sides*:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\cos(t)\\}\n= \\mathcal L\\left\\{\\dfrac{e^{\\mathrm{i} t} + e^{-\\mathrm{i} t}}{2}\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we apply linearity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}(t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,L{{e}^{it}+{e}^{-it}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(L{{e}^{it}}+L{{e}^{-it}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the previous lesson, we know that for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{\\mathrm{i} \\omega t}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-\\mathrm{i} \\omega}.\n$$\n\nSo for $\\omega=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{\\mathrm{i} t}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s-\\mathrm{i}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal L\\{e^{-\\mathrm{i} t}\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s+\\mathrm{i}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into our expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}(t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-i}+\\frac{1}{s+i}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+i)+(s-i)}{(s-i)(s+i)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}-{i}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{\\cos(t)\\} = \\dfrac{s}{s^2+1}, \\qquad s>0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9b027dd7-5bcc-5adb-b13e-81ee667c0551" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14724 :tutorial/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of Cosine Using Linearity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148689 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of exponential equations to extract helpful information from a process that undergoes exponential decay.\n\nSuppose, for example, that the vegetation coverage of a town is decreasing exponentially due to rapid urbanization, and that the vegetation coverage $C(t)$ (measured in acres) of the town varies according to the function\n\n$$\nC(t) = 48 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since urbanization began. Can we find how long it will take for the vegetation coverage to reduce to only $3$ acres?\n\nTo find the time taken for the vegetation cover to fall to $3$ acres, we need to solve the equation $C(t) = 3$. Setting up the equation, we have\n\n$$\n48 \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^t = 3\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first isolate the exponential term by dividing both sides by $48$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{48 {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{t}}{48}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{48}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{48} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{t}}{\\cancel{48}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{48}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{t} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3}{48}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{t} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{16}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $16 = 2^4$, and so we can rewrite the right-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{t} &= \\frac{1}{16} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{t} &= \\frac{1}{2^{4}} \\\\\n(\\frac{1}{2})^{t} &= (\\frac{1}{2})^{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation now have the same base, we can equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{t}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $4$ years for the vegetation cover to reduce to $3$ acres." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7c747ff-4cb7-5060-8ff8-9beb10f10a58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1998 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148750 :tutorial/content "The Laplace transform is a powerful tool for studying differential equations because it converts differentiation of a function $f(t)$ with respect to $t$ into algebraic operations in the variable $s$.\n\nIn this topic, we study how the Laplace transform interacts with derivatives of a function. This leads to another fundamental result, called the **derivative property** of the Laplace transform.\n\nLet's assume that:\n\n- $f(t)$ is *continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$,\n\n- $f'(t)$ is *piecewise continuous* on $[0,\\infty)$, and\n\n- $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a$.\n\nThen the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for all $s>a.$\n\nUnder these assumptions, the Laplace transform of the *first derivative* of $f$ satisfies a simple and extremely useful formula:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f'(t)\\} = sF(s) - f(0).\n$$\n\nThis formula shows that differentiating with respect to $t$ corresponds to multiplying by $s$ *and subtracting an initial-value term*.\n\nFor example, let $f(t) = t.$ We know $\\mathcal L\\{t\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s^2}$ and we also have $f(0)=0.$\nUsing our formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{'}(t)} & =sF(s)-f(0) \\\\  & =s(\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we know $f'(t) = 1$ and $\\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s}.$\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal L\\{f'(t)\\} = \\mathcal L\\{1\\} = \\dfrac{1}{s},\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that our formula gives the correct result in this case.\n\nWe can derive this formula from the definition of the Laplace transform. Let's see how." :tutorial/id #uuid "888142e4-d608-5aa2-a259-0d4a98f79c49" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4072 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148793 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of fraction multiplication to square fractions.\n\nFor example, let's evaluate the following squared fraction:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwith multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3}{5})^{2} = \\underbrace{\\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5}}_{{\\color{blue}2}\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the product of the two fractions. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{3}{5})^{2} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 \\cdot 3}{5 \\cdot 5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)^2= \\dfrac{9}{25}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f0e87aeb-338c-5f8d-be18-59b85ed28aac" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8806 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158508 :tutorial/content "When we have an exponential function in an inequality and the base of the exponential is *less* than $1,$ we can't take a logarithm of both sides using that same base. To see why not, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lt 1\n$$\n\nIf we take a logarithm with base $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ of both sides, then we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{\\frac{1}{2}}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & ≮{log}_{\\frac{1}{2}}⁡(1) \\\\ 1 & ≮0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can get around this problem by always taking a logarithm with a base that's *greater* than $1,$ such as the natural logarithm $\\ln,$ which has base $e \\approx 2.71 \\, .$ Then we can use the laws of logarithms to simplify the result.\n\nFor example, consider the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right)^x < 5\n$$\n\nWe can't take $\\log_\\frac{2}{3}$ of both sides, but we can take the natural logarithm of both sides and simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\ln 2 - \\ln 3$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d64947c7-c338-54b2-a5c3-a2b8a2cc504d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4657 :tutorial/title "The Case When the Base of the Exponential is Between Zero and One"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158522 :tutorial/content "We can also find the area of a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle if we're given the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nFirst, recall that in a $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, the length of the hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. Therefore, the length of a leg is the length of the hypotenuse *divided* by $\\sqrt{2}$.\n\nSo, if the hypotenuse has length $d$, then the legs have length\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{d}{\\sqrt 2}\n$$\n\n![](images/0c/0c4ced07bbb74fddaba2ddcbfbffcd2703da93d516eb8c86d3003485ef5be8d7.png)\n\nSo, the area of the $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle shown above is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{d}{\\sqrt{2}})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}d^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is some nice intuition behind the above formula: as shown below, the $45^\\circ$-$45^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle covers a quarter of a square with sides of length $d$.\n\n![](images/46/463200e7b002099fa856c3a6f706e1df63063c22fae386cbcd7a0eb7c85fd6e4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "5d03c8c3-5466-5fbe-b362-1402a1247307" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4676 :tutorial/title "The Area of a 45-45-90 Triangle in Terms of the Hypotenuse"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158539 :tutorial/content "The **variance** of a continuous random variable is the expected squared difference between the variable and its expected value.\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the variance is defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nIn practice, though, it's easier to use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm E [X^2] - \\textrm E[X]^2\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, consider the continuous random variable $X$ with the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{x}{4}, \\quad 1 < x < 3\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\textrm E[X]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{3}}{12}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we compute $\\textrm E[X^2]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{x}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5-{(\\frac{13}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24436a92-f030-567a-aa1c-938371716e3b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4805 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158558 :tutorial/content "Some rational numbers give a finite floating-point representation in some base and an infinite floating-point representation in another base.\n\nFor example, earlier, we saw that\n\n$$\n(0.1)_3 = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)_{10} = (0.333\\,333\\ldots)_{10}\n$$\n\nLet's try to convert the base $3$ number $(0.1)_3$, into a base-$4$ number.\n\nWe extract the powers of $4$ from the number (starting from the largest possible one):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(0.1)_{3} &= (\\frac{1}{3})_{10} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4^{-1} + \\frac{1}{12} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 4^{-2} + \\frac{1}{48} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 4^{-1} + 1 \\cdot 4^{-2} + 1 \\cdot 4^{-3} + \\frac{1}{192}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this process will never end (at each step the remainder fraction becomes one-fourth of the previous step). So,\n\n$$\n(0.1)_3 = (0.\\,111\\,111\\,\\ldots)_4\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "fd682e55-56cb-5945-99da-38e357d8582f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6008 :tutorial/title "Finite and Infinite Representations in Different Bases"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163206 :tutorial/content "Suppose a bag contains $10$ candies, $4$ of which are blue and $6$ are orange. We randomly select $3$ candies from the bag *without replacement* and count the number of blue candies.\n\nLet's assume we run this experiment and draw the following sequence:\n$\\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}},\\qquad {\\color{black}{\\textrm{orange}}},\\qquad \\stackrel{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}{\\textrm{blue}}$\n\nA diagram like the one shown below can help us visualize this process. It helps to imagine the bag containing two distinct sets of candies (blue and orange).\n\n![](images/c3/c3d4a81213cdbabeacca7e392ca2e2234df3f10c5682c7de8f5edeaa05de03cf.png)\n\nLet's interpret choosing a blue candy as a \"success\" and orange as a \"failure.\"\n\nLet the random variable $X$ equal the number of successes (blue candies in the sample). In the example above, we have $X=2$. The probability\n\n$$\nP(X=2)\n$$\n\nis given by the following ratio:\n\n$$\nP(X = 2) \n= \\dfrac{ {\\text{number of samples of size } {3} \\text{ with } {2} \\text{ blue candies}}}{ {\\text{number of samples of size } {3}}}\n$$\n\nThe denominator represents all possible ways to choose $3$ candies from $10$, while the numerator represents only those cases where exactly $2$ are blue.\n\nTo calculate this probability, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the number of samples of size $3$ containing $2$ blue candies:The number of ways of selecting $2$ blue candies from a total of $4$ in the population is given by\n$\\displaystyle {}_{4}C_{2} = \\binom{4}{2}$.\nThe number of ways of selecting $1$ orange candy from a total of $6$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{6}C_{1} = \\binom{6}{1}$.\nSo, by the *rule of product,* the number of samples of size $3$ with precisely $2$ blue candies is\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle \\text{# of ways of selecting } {2} \\text{ blue candies} \\hspace{0.222em} \\times  \\hspace{0.222em} \\text{# of ways of selecting } {1} \\text{ orange candy} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{2} \\right) \\cdot  \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1} \\right) .\\end{array}}\n$$.\n(Note. The symbol $\\textrm{#}$ is shorthand for \"number of\").\n- **Step 2**: Determine the total number of samples of size $3$.This is simply the number of ways of selecting $3$ objects from a population of $10$. This is given by\n${}_{10}C_{3} = \\binom{10}{3}$.\n- **Step 3**: Divide the results.Therefore, we conclude that\n$P(X = 2)|= \\frac{# of samples of size [math]3[/math] with precisely [math]2[/math] blue candies}{# of samples of size [math]3[/math]}; = ((\\frac{4}{2}) \\cdot (\\frac{6}{1}))/((\\frac{10}{3})); = \\frac{3}{10}$.\n\nThis same reasoning applies in more general situations where we sample without replacement. If we have a population of $N$ objects, where $K$ are \"successes,\" and we randomly draw $n$ objects, then the number of successes follows a so-called **hypergeometric distribution,** and we write\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(N, K, n)\n$$\n\nIn our example, we had\n$X\\sim \\textrm{Hypergeometric}(10, 4, 3)$,\nsince there were $N=10$ candies, $K=4$ blue candies (successes), and we were considering samples of size $n=3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "2d85a9b5-b913-59b9-8314-47ad73ebfded" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10876 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163296 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to conduct chi-square goodness-of-fit tests to determine whether some sample data fit a binomial distribution.\n\nFirst, let's remind ourselves of some basic facts.\n\n- Given a random variable $X\\sim B(n, p)$, the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n$P(X = x)|= (\\frac{n}{x})p^{x}(1 - p)^{n - x}$.\n- Given a random sample of size $N$ drawn from $X\\sim B(n,p)$, the expected frequency of the outcome $X=x$ is given by\n$E_x = N\\cdot P(X=x)$.\n\nLet's get some practice at computing expected values using the binomial distribution." :tutorial/id #uuid "42ba5800-f73f-5b21-84d2-447f39e6eefc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5656 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163339 :tutorial/content "Suppose the continuous random variable $X$ is defined as\n\n![](images/88/88103878042469595fc8f8243958b44b128c0d38465bfe93a31f83afd6988300.svg)\nLet $Y= 1+2X.$ Let's use the distribution function method to find the probability density function of $Y.$\n\nFirst, note that $Y = 1+2X$ is a strictly increasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $x > 0,$ we have $y > 1.$\n\n![](images/54/544e551755f4c8a575ef227b77fe520deec203f08654eec31109e496141aa91c.png)\n\nLet's consider $y > 1.$ We write down the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ and then use our transformation to isolate $X$ in the parentheses. This will allow us to write $F_Y$ in terms of $F_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X+1\\le y) \\\\  & =P(2X\\le y-1) \\\\  & =P(X\\le \\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the PDF $f_Y(y)$ for $y > 1,$ we differentiate $F_Y$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({F}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\frac{y-1}{2})\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of $f_X,$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(\\frac{y-1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{-(y-1)/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{(1-y)/2}, & \\,y>1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's easy (and often worthwhile) to check that this is a valid probability density function." :tutorial/id #uuid "6eea312a-622e-5ce1-ae71-45e4cc089144" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6387 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163348 :tutorial/content "Let's find the conditional expectation $\\textrm{E}\\big[X \\,|\\, Y=1\\big]$ given that the conditional probability density function $f_{X|Y}(x \\,|\\, 1)$ is given by\n![](images/8b/8b71da7aecae81ed4e8d9dc27f4730d3d0a03d73146b7b899003d3f8787694bc.svg) \n\nApplying the formula for the conditional expectation of $X$ given $Y=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X\\,|\\,Y=1] & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot 2(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x(x-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{x}^{2}-2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2(2)^{3}}{3}-(2)^{2}]-[\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{3}-(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-4]-[\\frac{2}{3}-1] \\\\  & =[\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{12}{3}]-[\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c2314f8-7929-53f2-9261-e2cc145337ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6481 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163371 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a coin that we suspect is biased *against* landing on heads (i.e., the coin is *less* likely to land on heads than tails). We wish to design a hypothesis test to determine whether or not our suspicions are correct.\n\nWe can use a so-called **left-tailed** hypothesis test for this purpose. To do that, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Determine the population parameter we're interested in. $\\qquad$ *We define $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"*\n- **Step 2**: Define our hypotheses:*Since we want to check whether the coin is biased **against** landing on heads, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:*\n$H_{0}: p|= \\frac{1}{2}; H_{1}: p \\mid < \\frac{1}{2}$We use a \"less than\" symbol (the left tail of the probability distribution) for $H_1$ because if the coin is biased against heads, then the probability that the coin lands on heads must be less than one-half.\n- **Step 3**: Design a suitable experiment to test our hypotheses. $\\qquad$ *We toss our coin $10$ times and count the total number of heads.*\n- **Step 4**: Set a significance level $\\alpha$. $\\qquad$ *For our experiment, let's pick the significance level $\\alpha = 5\\%$.*\n- **Step 5**: Define and calculate a test statistic.In our case, we might define a suitable test statistic as follows:$\\qquad X=$ \"*the number of times the coin lands on heads after $10$ random tosses*.\"Notice that under the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ must follow a binomial distribution:\n$X \\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)$ Suppose we toss the coin $10$ times and get only ${\\color{red}{1}}$ head. If the null hypothesis is true, then\n$P(X \\le 1)|= P(X = 0) + P(X = 1); = (\\frac{10}{0})(\\frac{1}{2})^{0}(\\frac{1}{2})^{10} + (\\frac{10}{1})(\\frac{1}{2})^{1}(\\frac{1}{2})^{9}; \\approx 0.0107; = 1.07%$.\n- **Step 6**: Draw a conclusion.The probability $P(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})$ is *smaller than* our significance level of $5\\%$. This means that our result *is* statistically significant. In other words, it is unlikely that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true. Therefore, we should *reject* the null hypothesis and conclude that the coin is biased.\n\nYou might be wondering why our test statistic was\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq {\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nrather than\n\n$$\nP(X={\\color{red}{1}})\n$$\n\nWhen conducting hypothesis tests, we consider not just the observed outcome, but all outcomes that are *at least as extreme*—meaning outcomes that point even more strongly toward the alternative hypothesis. Since our alternative hypothesis is\n\n$$\np < \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nlower values of $X$ provide stronger evidence for it. The cumulative probability\n\n$$\nP(X\\leq 1)\n$$\n\naccounts for $X = 1$ and the even more extreme outcome $X = 0$.\n\n**Right-tailed** hypothesis tests are similar. The difference is the alternative hypothesis involves a \"greater than\" sign. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "ae96a360-e5e1-582e-b689-325d312c093e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9444 :tutorial/title "Carrying Out a Hypothesis Test"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168027 :tutorial/content "Next, we evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n![](images/d5/d57e5d0566616dcf983c8d2d9ee8375cb70aa44d4d5933026266396202248d22.svg)\nat the nonzero equilibrium ![](images/b3/b3e4462dae63d3e4cbaddcd890e1553dd26372c2fdca8efe87cbfb8984b9a492.svg) to get\n\n![](images/ec/ecc7fb063630624a8acc4ab201208c2cfb86fe97b910cdc6e17fb71629299385.svg)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & -\\frac{bc}{d} \\\\ \\frac{ad}{b} & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )\\cdot (-\\lambda )-(-\\frac{bc}{d})\\cdot \\frac{ad}{b} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+ac & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues \n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm \\sqrt{ac}\\,\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a *stable center*.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model, it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues.\nHowever, we will not go into detail on why in this lesson.\n\nJust keep in mind that for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone would be inconclusive in this case (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source).\n\nTherefore, a typical phase portrait of the system looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/23/236a29ae07d8fd091fd602ad124af53b49754419b949bd42713078bc9d8814b6.png)\n\nThe global picture in the first quadrant is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions which intersect at the saddle point $(0,0){:}$\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach infinity (unstable line). Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey increases exponentially.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin (stable line). Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories circle around the nonzero equilibrium point $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d},\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73373dc9-48c3-5ba7-b785-32651f0f7563" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14593 :tutorial/title "Local Behavior Near the Nonzero Equilibrium"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168028 :tutorial/content "Recall that the equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, for the Lotka–Volterra model \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nthe equilibria are the solutions of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bxy &=0 \\\\ -cy+dxy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-cy+dxy & =0 \\\\ y(-c+dx) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=\\dfrac{c}{d}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax(1-hx)-bx(0) & =0 \\\\ ax(1-hx) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{1}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$\n\n- If $x=\\dfrac{c}{d},$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{c}{d})(1-h(\\frac{c}{d}))-b(\\frac{c}{d})y & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h)-by & =0 \\\\ a(1-\\frac{c}{d}h) & =by \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{b}(1-\\frac{c}{d}h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1-\\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79e78f76-a29e-544f-a86b-9f714e69cf63" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14597 :tutorial/title "Equilibrium Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168087 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that the position of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t), z(t) \\right\\rangle.$$\n\nRecall that the unit tangent vector is given by\n\n$$\\mathbf T = \\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf r}{\\textrm{d}t}}{\\left\\Vert\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf r}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right\\Vert} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v}}{||\\mathbf{v}||}.$$\n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\\mathbf{v} = ||\\mathbf{v}||\\mathbf T.$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\\mathbf{a} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}t}(||\\mathbf{v}||)\\mathbf T + ||\\mathbf{v}||\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}t}.$$\n\nNow, from the definition of the normal vector $\\mathbf{N}$, notice that\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\left\\Vert\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf T}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right\\Vert\\mathbf N = \\kappa ||\\mathbf{v}||\\mathbf N,$$\n\nwhere $\\kappa$ is the curvature. Putting this into our equation for acceleration, we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(||v||)T+||v||\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(||v||)T+||v||(κ||v||N) \\\\ & =\\underbrace{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(||v||)}_{{a}_{T}}T+\\underbrace{κ||v|{|}^{2}}_{{a}_{N}}N \\\\ & ={a}_{T}T+{a}_{N}N.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThis means that the acceleration of the particle lies in the osculating plane spanned by $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N},$ where the tangential component is the coefficient of $\\mathbf{T}$ and the normal component is the coefficient of $\\mathbf{N}.$\n\nIndeed, taking the dot product of $\\mathbf{T}$ with $\\mathbf{a}$ gives\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot a & =T\\cdot ({a}_{T}T+{a}_{N}N) \\\\ & ={a}_{T}(T\\cdot T)+{a}_{N}(T\\cdot N) \\\\ & ={a}_{T}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$a_T = \\mathbf{T} \\cdot \\mathbf{a} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{a}}{||\\mathbf{v}||}.$$\n\nAlso, crossing $\\mathbf{T}$ with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}T\\times a & =T\\times ({a}_{T}T+{a}_{N}N) \\\\ & ={a}_{T}(T\\times T)+{a}_{N}(T\\times N) \\\\ & ={a}_{N}(T\\times N).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTaking the norm of both sides and remembering that $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ are orthogonal unit vectors, we have that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}||T\\times a|| & =||{a}_{N}(T\\times N)|| \\\\ & ={a}_{N}||T\\times N|| \\\\ & ={a}_{N}||T||||N||\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ & ={a}_{N}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\ & ={a}_{N}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$a_N = ||\\mathbf{T} \\times \\mathbf{a}|| = \\dfrac{||\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{a}||}{||\\mathbf{v}||}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7322be9a-212a-5a8e-b381-6b19fc56a3fb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5173 :tutorial/title "Explanation of the Components of Acceleration"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168098 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-10x+34}}\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can complete the square for the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 10x + 34 &= x^{2} - 10x + (25 - 25) + 34 \\\\\n&= (x^{2} - 10x + 25) - 25 + 34 \\\\\n&= (x - 5)^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of $9$ in the denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 10x + 34}}dx &= \\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(x - 5)^{2} + 9}} \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{9[\\frac{(x - 5)^{2}}{9} + 1]}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{x - 5}{3})^{2} + 1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis now looks more familiar! Let\n\n$$\nu = \\dfrac {x-5}{3}\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}x = 3\\,\\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^{2} - 10x + 34}}dx &= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\sqrt{(\\frac{x - 5}{3})^{2} + 1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}} \\cdot (3du) \\\\\n&= \\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}}du\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a basic integral for an inverse hyperbolic function, and we can solve it in one step:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u^{2} + 1}}du &= \\operatorname{arsinh} u + C \\\\\n&= \\operatorname{arsinh} (\\frac{x - 5}{3}) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "59b06095-0ed1-54ec-b486-9618bb3c0704" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5217 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168099 :tutorial/content "We can also complete the square to integrate hyperbolic *arcsecant*, *arccosecant*, and *arccotangent*.\n\nFor example, let's consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\left(x+3\\right)\\sqrt{-x^2-6x}} \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nAs it is, this integral doesn't resemble any of the basic integrals we have encountered so far. However, we can complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-6x & =-({x}^{2}+6x) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =-((x+3)^{2}-9) \\\\  & =9-(x+3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we plug the above into our integral and collect a factor of $9$ under the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{9-(x+3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{3(x+3)\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis now looks more familiar! Let $u = \\dfrac {x+3}{3}.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}x = 3\\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x+3}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (3\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a basic integral for an inverse reciprocal hyperbolic function, and we can solve it in one step:\n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5f0c7466-a41d-5101-b125-8e6f68fba3ea" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5218 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168101 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$ \n\nWe wish to prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sech{x}) = -\\sech{x} \\tanh{x}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sech x = \\dfrac{1}{\\cosh x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation using the chain rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sech}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}^{-1} \\\\  & =-{(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)}^{-2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x)^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{cosh}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sech}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can prove the results for the derivatives of the hyperbolic cosecant and cotangent in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "cb6ff220-98cb-5e8a-8a09-958d158b0d10" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5226 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Result for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Secant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168102 :tutorial/content "Recall that \n$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{blue}\\artanh{x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{blue}x\\in(-1,1)}.\n$$\n\nBut we have just seen that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1-x^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x = {\\color{red}\\arcoth{x}} + C, \\qquad {\\color{red}x\\in(-\\infty,-1) \\cup (1,\\infty)}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the integral on the left-hand side is the same but we used different functions on the right. What is the difference, and how do we know which hyperbolic function to use?\n\nThe difference is the domain of the variable $x.$ When the $|x| < 1,$ we use the antiderivative $\\artanh{x},$ otherwise we use the antiderivative $\\arcoth{x}.$\n\nFor instance, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x\\,{|}_{-1/2}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(-1,1),$ we used the antiderivative $\\artanh{x}.$\n\nWhile, on the other hand, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡x\\,{|}_{2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(3)-\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since the limits of integration lie in the interval $(1,\\infty),$ we used the antiderivative $\\arcoth{x}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "169c2d19-f0a1-557e-88ed-519503440186" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5227 :tutorial/title "The Importance of the Domain When Integrating Using Inverse Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168148 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the horizontal plane $z=k,$ where $k$ is a fixed parameter. \n\nThe magnitude of the fundamental vector product is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}|| & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂k}{∂y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in such cases, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,f(x,y,z)\\text{d}S\\, & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot ||{r}_{x}\\times {r}_{y}||\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRestating, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z)\\, \\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nWe arrive at a similar result when considering vertical planes parallel to the $xz$- or $yz$-axes.\n\nThis observation can help provide a shortcut when calculating the flux of a vector field through a horizontal plane. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3a45ce20-02a0-56e8-9b38-add2ba9480d1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7733 :tutorial/title "The Fundamental Vector Product for a Horizontal Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173109 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample to find the average weekly cost commuters spend traveling to work in a certain city. Let's assume we initially used a sample of size $n_1$ to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nAfter completing the initial survey, the city planners decided that a new sample must be carried out, and the resulting confidence interval should have the following improvements:\n\n- The confidence level of the new confidence interval should be $99\\%$.\n- The new confidence interval should be one-third as wide as the original (increased precision).\n\nFor a fixed sample size, increasing the confidence level leads to an *increase* in the width of the confidence interval. In other words, precision is reduced when we increase the confidence level. To increase precision, we need to *increase* the sample size.\n\nLet's determine how much larger the second sample size $n_2$ should be compared to the first to satisfy these new conditions.\n\nFirst, recall that, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe define the following:\n\n- Let $I_1$ and $I_2$ be the $95\\%$ and $99\\%$ confidence intervals for the population mean obtained from samples of size $n_1$ and $n_2$, respectively.\n- Let $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively.\n\nSince we want $I_2$ to be one-third of the length of $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = 3\\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n_1}} = 3 \\cdot z_{0.005} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n_2}}\n$$\n\nCanceling $\\sigma$ and solving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= 3 \\cdot z_{0.005} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n_{2}}} \\\\\n\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= \\frac{3 \\cdot z_{0.005}}{z_{0.025}} \\\\\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}})^{2} &= (\\frac{3 \\cdot z_{0.005}}{z_{0.025}})^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{9 \\cdot z_{0.005}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\nz_{0.005} = 2.576\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} = 1.960\n$$\n\n, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{9 \\cdot z_{0.005}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9 \\cdot 2.576^{2}}{1.960^{2}} \\\\\n&\\approx 15.54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\nn_2 \\approx 15.5 n_1\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be larger than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $15.5$. Note that the additional time and resources needed to conduct a sample that's more than fifteen times the size of the first could be significant!\n\nLet's take a look at a similar problem in the case where the population variance is unknown." :tutorial/id #uuid "8e4a111e-b7ce-5bad-8d48-67c7f9323677" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10559 :tutorial/title "Factors Affecting Sample Size"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173120 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample to find the proportion of voters who intend to vote for a particular candidate in an upcoming election. Let's assume we initially used a sample of size $n_1$ to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nAfter completing the initial survey, it's decided that a new sample must be carried out, and the resulting confidence interval should have the following features:\n\n- The confidence level of the new confidence interval should be $95\\%$ (unchanged).\n- The new confidence interval should be one-half as wide as the original (increased precision).\n\nTo increase the precision while keeping the confidence level fixed, we'll need to *increase* the sample size. But by how much? Let's determine how much larger the second sample size $n_2$ should be compared to the first to satisfy the new conditions.\n\nFirst, recall that, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n- $p$ is the population proportion,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nGiven a particular margin of error $\\varepsilon$, we can deduce an approximate sample size:\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}} = \\varepsilon \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad n = \\dfrac{z_{\\alpha/2}^2 \\, \\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of the confidence intervals $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to half the length of $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = 2\\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn_{1} &= (z_{α/2}^{2}\\widehat{p}(1 - \\widehat{p}))/(ε_{1}^{2}), \\\\\nn_{2} &= (z_{α/2}^{2}\\widehat{p}(1 - \\widehat{p}))/(ε_{2}^{2}), \\\\\n\\frac{n_{1}}{n_{2}} &= \\frac{ε_{2}^{2}}{ε_{1}^{2}} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{ε_{2}}{ε_{1}})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{2})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n4 n_1 = n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{larger}}$ then $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}4}$." :tutorial/id #uuid "10e10ea8-01a6-5d1c-8993-4d6fefbe2a38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10765 :tutorial/title "Factors Affecting Sample Size"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173162 :tutorial/content "Consider the unit circle configured in the usual way in the coordinate plane. Suppose a particle $P$ is initially at the point $(1,0)$ and moves counterclockwise along the unit circle with a fixed **angular velocity** of $\\dfrac\\pi3$ **radians per second.** \n\nThe first three seconds of the particle's motion are illustrated below.\n\n![](images/00/009b83d5d0e48c6983950a48523838cc569b3a92511dc9362630316b1d88f86b.png)\n\n    \n\nSince the angular velocity is constant, we refer to this as **uniform circular motion**. This fixed angular velocity is denoted $\\omega,$ and we write\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac\\pi 3\\,\\textrm{rad/s}.\n$$\n\nNow, let $\\mathbf r(t)$ be the position vector of $P$ at time $t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi t}{3}\\right) \\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nIt's worth building a picture of why this formula is true:\n\n- We know that $\\mathbf r(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta\\,\\mathbf i + \\sin\\theta\\,\\mathbf j$ traces out a unit circle, starting at the point $(1,0).$\n\n- We can see from the picture that $P$ will return to the starting position after $t=6$ seconds, so the angular frequency of the motion is\n\n$$\n\\omega = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{6} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{3})\\,j\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $P$ indeed returns to its starting location when $t=6{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(6) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{3})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,j \\\\  & =r(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, if a particle moves counterclockwise along a circle of radius $R$ centered at $O$ with initial position $(R,0),$ then its position vector $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf {r}(t)= R\\left(\\cos\\omega t \\, \\mathbf i + \\sin\\omega t \\,\\mathbf j\\right)\\!\\,.\n$$\n\nNote that for clockwise motion, we have $\\omega < 0.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "a0eebf3c-594c-5d34-868e-6c476f587498" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5092 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173203 :tutorial/content "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y,$ respectively, corresponding to the change in $t.$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t.\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}](Δt)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s\n\n\\approx\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65578a76-05f7-551a-acbd-956ae6831965" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5802 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Parametric Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173204 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve $r= r(\\theta)$ defined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r,$ and\n\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'.$ The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ≈(Δr)^{2}+(rΔ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =Δ{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =[{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}](Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b310dc84-9da7-5492-9639-145086fe7403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5803 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Polar Curve"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173208 :tutorial/content "Green's theorem allows us to write line integrals as double integrals, and we've seen how Green's theorem simplifies circulation calculations. Even more, Green's theorem can also be used to simplify flux calculations!\n\nTo see how, we first recall that the flux of $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) =  P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\mathbf j$ across $C$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s= \\oint\\limits_C P\\, \\textrm{d}y -Q\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is closed and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nNow, let's set $M= -Q$ and $N=P$. Then, by the circulation form of Green's theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x & ={∮}_{C}N\\,\\text{d}y+M\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}M\\,\\text{d}x+N\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂N}{∂x}-\\frac{∂M}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}-(-\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}))\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we've obtained the **Green's theorem in flux form**, which we restate below:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} +\\dfrac{\\partial  Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F =  \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can also write Green's theorem in flux form as\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F\\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form allows us to convert a flux line integral into an equivalent double integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "7f3b4b70-8b58-53d1-bc8d-a42daddfab4d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5868 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173222 :tutorial/content "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, |S|\\}.\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}f(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot {\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot |S|\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\cancel{|S|}\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+|S|}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-{(\\text{E}[X])}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-{(\\frac{|S|+1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-\\frac{(|S|+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{12}-\\frac{3(|S|+1)^{2}}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(2(2|S|+1)-3(|S|+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(4|S|+2-3|S|-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(|S|-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(|S|-1)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :tutorial/id #uuid "3ac5d5b0-adb4-5096-93dc-1a400ec42c5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6459 :tutorial/title "Proving the Formula for the Variance"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173224 :tutorial/content "Here, we'll prove the recursive formula for the gamma function:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z)\n$$\n\nThe formula comes from applying integration by parts to the integral in the definition of the gamma function. By definition,\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z) = \\int_0^\\infty t^{z-1} e^{-t} \\, \\textrm dt, \\qquad\\textrm{Re}(z) > 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(z+1) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{z}{e}^{-t}{|}_{0}^{\\infty }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }-z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})-{(-\\frac{{t}^{z}}{{e}^{t}})|}_{t=0}+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }z{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0-0+z\\underbrace{{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t}_{Γ(z)} \\\\  & =zΓ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68b4463b-d790-5bf1-9972-da35d3818a8b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6699 :tutorial/title "Proof of the Recursive Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173281 :tutorial/content "**Note**: You may find the algebra in this section a little tricky. It's a good idea to go through it, but you will not be quizzed on it. You may skip this section if you wish.\n\n![](images/2a/2a400b12675f8ebf19ba563192d9ae2296336e40ea380d40e6247564718a2b1f.png)\n\nYou may have seen already that for the parabola\n\n$$\ny=ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nthe **axis of symmetry** of this parabola is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe're now in a position to derive this formula.\n\nSince\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nwe can take out a factor of $a$ from the right-hand side in the parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny = a\\left(x^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + \\dfrac{c}{a} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2+{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2}_{\\textrm{perfect square}} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{b}{2a}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{c}{a}}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{b^2-4ac}{4a^2} = \\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a} \\right)^2 + \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a^2}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na((x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}}) &= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + a \\cdot \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a^{2}} \\\\\n&= a(x + \\frac{b}{2a})^{2} + \\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a} \\\\\n&= a(x - \\underbrace{(-\\frac{b}{2a})}_{h})^{2} + \\underbrace{\\frac{4ac - b^{2}}{4a}}_{k}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy denoting\n\n$$\nh=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\qquad k=\\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a}\n$$\n\nwe get the equation of the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\ny = a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nwhere the vertex is given by\n\n$$\n(h,k) = \\left(-\\dfrac{b}{2a}, \\dfrac{4ac-b^2}{4a} \\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, since the parabola's axis of symmetry is the vertical line passing through the vertex, the equation of the axis must be\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "41e62e06-e589-55dc-846e-1177555ce582" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9116 :tutorial/title "Derivation of the Axis of Symmetry Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176978 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the following infinite power series.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2} = (x-2) + \\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{(x-2)^3}{3^2} + \\cdots\n$$\n\nHow do we determine the radius and interval of convergence for this series?\n\nFirst, note that the terms of the series are all powers of $(x-2).$ For this reason, we say that the series is **centered** at $x=2.$\n\nTo compute the radius of convergence, we use the ratio test for convergence. We define \n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{(x-2)^n}{n^2},\n$$\n\nand we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{2}}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{(n+1)^{2}}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}+2n+1}) \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}) \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x-2|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if \n\n$$\nL < {\\color{blue}1}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\left| x-{\\color{red}2} \\right| < {\\color{blue}1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the radius of convergence is ${\\color{blue}1},$ and\n\n- the interval of convergence is centered at the point $x={\\color{red}2}.$\n\nTo find the interval of convergence, we solve the inequality $\\left| x-2 \\right| < 1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x-2| & <1 \\\\ -1<x-2 & <1 \\\\ 1<x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the series converges for any $x$ in the open interval $(1,3).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series at the endpoints of this interval. So, we need to investigate the endpoints $x=1$ and $x=3$ separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- We start with the left endpoint. When $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(1-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- We then look at the right endpoint. When $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(3-2)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{16}+⋯,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the $p$-series test.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the interval of convergence is $I=[1,3].$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3711e5cb-ff53-5f13-a62a-da24f59e888b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5191 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176990 :tutorial/content "![](images/86/867722106ffd8445b62a98ab43197ac2fba646faff95017ef7e92c254a97fb6e.png)\n\n  \n\nThe surface $S$ shown above consists of the part of the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$ that lies under the plane $z=9.$ Let's compute the surface area of $S.$\n\nThe plane intersects the paraboloid when $z=9.$ Thus, the curve that describes the intersection points is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2 = 9,\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ in the $xy$-plane. \n\nTherefore, our surface can be described by the equations\n\n$$\nz = f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2, \\quad (x,y) \\in D\n$$\n\nwhere the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{(x,y): \\, x^2 + y^2 \\le 9\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the surface area of $S,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S = \\iint\\limits_D \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions into our surface area formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+(2x)^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by changing the variables to polar coordinates $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta),$ we have $\\textrm dA = r\\,\\textrm d r\\, \\textrm d \\theta.$ Thus, our integration region becomes\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{(r,\\theta): \\, 0\\leq r\\leq 3,\\:0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{1+4(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr\\,d\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,d\\theta \\,{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{1+4{r}^{2}\\,}\\,dr \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{1}{12}{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}{[{(1+4{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}(37\\sqrt{37}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the area of our surface is \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}(37\\sqrt{37} -1).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1f3795a6-1971-55b3-9109-8565cd3830a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5344 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177007 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $X$ is a random variable. We know that the variance of $X$ can be expressed in terms of the raw moments $\\textrm E[X]$ and $\\textrm E[X^2]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nLet $M(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X$. Since\n\n$$\n\\textrm E [X] = M'(0), \\qquad \\textrm E [X^2] = M''(0)\n$$\n\nwe can express the variance of $X$ in terms of derivatives of the MGF evaluated at zero:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] =M''(0) - [M'(0)]^2\n$$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ have the following probability distribution:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{3}} , & x = 0 \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3}} , & x = 1\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nThen, the MGF is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(t) &= f(0)e^{0t} + f(1)e^{t} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the first derivative and the second derivative of MGF, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}, \\\\\nM^{″}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(M^{′}(t)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{3}e^{t}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= M^{″}(0) - (M^{′}(0))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - (\\frac{1}{3})^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{9}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02f648cc-8ecf-5c4f-bfe9-eb5c570473db" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5530 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177054 :tutorial/content "![](images/7a/7a1647aa543ef6b9c4c2004e358a91b8d034bd8d72cc53e72bf0dc69266366bc.png)\n\nTo find the horizontal tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $y'(\\theta)=0.$\n\nWriting down the equation $y'(\\theta) = 0$ and factoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0, \\qquad \\cos\\theta + 1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta-1 = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta + 1=0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solution to this equation is \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have three candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $x'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into the expression for $x'(\\theta).$ If $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.  \n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+1) \\\\  & =-0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_2 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have horizontal tangents where $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}.$ These correspond to the points $A$ and $B,$ respectively.\n\n- This analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons.\n\nFor the remainder of this lesson, we will not concern ourselves with the case where $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta)$ are both zero. However, it's worth carrying out step 3 regardless when solving these problems, as this will help develop good habits that will come in handy later." :tutorial/id #uuid "c2158ee2-ecde-50ce-b68e-b23dde7e3b42" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6268 :tutorial/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents to Polar Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177061 :tutorial/content "![](images/20/20b8d6a35a9925c0442830f0abd20c522f0e3a420d27e6239e43120edbfc55d3.png)\n\nTo find the vertical tangents, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Compute the derivatives $y'(\\theta)$ and $x'(\\theta).$ \n\nDoing this using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the values of $\\theta$ where $x'(\\theta)=0{:}$\n\nWriting down the equation $x'(\\theta) = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the zero-product property, we have to solve two equations: \n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0, \\qquad 2\\cos\\theta+1= 0\n$$\n\nLet's solve each equation separately.\n\n- First, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = 0,  \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are \n\n$$\n\\theta = 0,\\pi.\n$$\n\n- Then, we solve the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cos\\theta+1= 0, \\qquad 0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have four candidate solutions:\n\n$$\n\\theta_1 =0, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}, \\qquad \\theta_3 = \\pi, \\qquad \\theta_2 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Check to see whether any candidates have $y'(\\theta) = 0.$ \n\nWe substitute our candidate solutions into $y'(\\theta)$ and check that $y'(\\theta) \\neq 0.$ If $y'(\\theta) = 0,$ further investigation is necessary.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_1 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)-1 \\\\  & =2+1-1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_1 = 0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_2 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_3 = \\pi,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2}-1-1 \\\\  & =2-1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we draw *no conclusion* regarding $\\theta_3 = \\pi.$\n\n- Substituting $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}) & =2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta_4 = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ is a valid solution.\n\nWe now make the following conclusions: \n\n- We have vertical tangents where $\\theta = 0, \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$ These correspond to the points $P, Q,$ and $R,$ respectively.\n\n- Again, this analysis gave no conclusion regarding the point where $\\theta = \\pi.$ We'll learn to deal with points like this in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "afebb67f-3a1e-5c0a-95b1-9a5065f21f09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6386 :tutorial/title "Finding Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177087 :tutorial/content "We know how to find $2^{-1}.$ But what about $2^{-2}, 2^{-3},2^{-4},\\ldots$?\n\nTo work out ${\\color{blue}{2}}^{{\\color{red}{-2}}},$ all we need to do is find the reciprocal of the base $({\\color{blue}{2}})$ and then raise that reciprocal to the same power but with the *opposite* sign $({{\\color{red}{+2}}})$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2})\\times (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1\\times 1}{2\\times 2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA similar approach can be used for other powers. Let's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "d6f8e85f-3130-5d56-9648-0225b32a7478" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8025 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180691 :tutorial/content "The mass $M(t)$ of a radioactive substance is given by\n\n$$\nM(t) = M_0 e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nThe half-life of a substance is the time taken for the mass of a sample to reduce by a factor of one-half. Therefore, it is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12 M_0 = M_0 e^{-kt}.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{M}_{0} & ={M}_{0}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{M}_{0}} & =\\cancel{{M}_{0}}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-kt} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{-kt}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}1-\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-kt \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}2 & =-kt \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}2}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the half-life of a substance is given by the formula\n\n$$\nt_{1/2} = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{k}.\n$$\n\nEvery radioactive substance has a half-life associated with it. It depends only on the substance itself and is independent of its initial mass of the sample (we know this because we were able to cancel $M_0$ in our derivation of $t_{1/2}$). \n\nIt's worth noting that half-lives show significant variation in magnitude across different substances. Some substances that decay very rapidly have half-lives on the order of nanoseconds. On the other hand, some substances that decay very slowly have half-lives that are on the order of billions of years." :tutorial/id #uuid "cda969b3-51ce-59ee-9fc7-21187fd87887" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5721 :tutorial/title "Deriving a Formula for the Half-Life"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180695 :tutorial/content "Now let's suppose that the investor pays $\\[math]5\\,000$ *per year* into an investment account that generates a return of $8\\%$, compounded annually. Let $S_n$ be the value of the investment at the end of the $n$th year. Our goal is to calculate $S_{30}$, the value of the portfolio when the investor retires in $30$ years.\n\nLet's work out $S_n$ for\n\n$$\nn=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nand see if we can spot a pattern.\n\n- At the end of year $1$, the investment will be worth\n$S_1 = \\[/math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)$.\n- To calculate the value of the investment at the end of year $2$, we first need to add $\\[math]5\\,000$ to the balance at the end of year $1$. This gives the expression\n$S_1 + [/math]5\\,000$.\nThen, we increase the amount by $8 \\%$. Therefore, at the end of the year $2$, the investment will be worth\n$S_2 = \\big(S_1 + [math]5\\,000\\big) \\cdot (1.08)$.\nSubstituting our expression for $S_1$ and expanding the parentheses, we get\n$S_{2}|= (\\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08) + \\[math]5000) \\cdot (1.08); = \\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\[math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)$.\n- Similarly, at the end of the year $3$, the investment will be worth\n$S_{3}|= (S_{2} + \\[/math]5000) \\cdot (1.08); = [(\\[math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)) + \\[math]5000] \\cdot (1.08); = \\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{3} + \\[math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)^{2} + \\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08)$.\n\nYou can probably spot the pattern now. We can deduce that the expression for $S_{30}$ is equal to\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\[math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{30} + \\[/math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08)^{29} + \\cdots + {5 \\hspace{0.167em} 000 \\cdot  ( 1.08 {)}^{2} +}5\\,000\\cdot (1.08).\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this sum, we first factor it as follows:\n\n$$\nS_{30} = \\[math]5\\,000\\cdot (1.08) \\cdot \\left[ (1.08)^{29} +(1.08)^{28} + \\cdots + (1.08) + 1 \\right]\n$$\n\nNotice that the expression inside the square brackets is a geometric series with $a_1 = 1$, $r = 1.08$, and $N = 30$. Thus, after $30$ years, the investment will be worth\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{30} &= \\[/math]5000 \\cdot (1.08) \\cdot ((a_{1}(1 - r^{N}))/(1 - r)) \\\\\n&= \\[math]5400 \\cdot ((1 \\cdot (1 - 1.08^{30}))/(1 - 1.08)) \\\\\n&= \\[/math]5400 \\cdot (\\frac{1 - 1.08^{30}}{1 - 1.08}) \\\\\n&\\approx \\[math]611729\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest dollar.\n\nThis example illustrates how compound interest can be used to fund retirements. The investor deposited a total of\n\n$$\n\\[/math]5\\,000\\cdot 30 = \\[math]150\\,000\n$$\n\nover $30$ years, yet this was worth $\\[/math]611\\,729$ by the time they retired!" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a8c520b-d1ab-53c0-ab94-bef977f0f99e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5754 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Value of an Investment With Annual Compounding"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180728 :tutorial/content "Let's now derive the following formula for the flux:\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_C P\\,\\textrm{d}y- Q\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that if $C$ is parameterized by the function $\\mathbf r(t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ then \n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{y'(t)\\,\\mathbf i - x'(t)\\,\\mathbf j}{||\\mathbf r'(t)||},\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that $C$ is positively-oriented. \n\nSubstituting our expression for $\\mathbf n$ into our line integral definition of flux and using the fact that $\\textrm d s = ||\\mathbf r'(t)|| \\,\\textrm d t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{||{r}^{′}(t)||})||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j}{\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}})\\cancel{||{r}^{′}(t)||}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot ({y}^{′}(t)\\,i-{x}^{′}(t)\\,j)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P(x(t),y(t)){y}^{′}(t)-Q(x(t),y(t)){x}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cc71a06d-4832-5949-91b4-71cb0f8185ca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5870 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Main Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180729 :tutorial/content "We can use well-known series to recover the sequence associated with a generating function.\n\nOne series that occurs regularly for this purpose is the geometric series\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1-r} = 1+r+r^2+r^3+\\cdots\\,\n$$\n\nFor example, let's determine the general term $a_n$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 0\n$$\n\nof the sequence with generating function\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5+2x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the generating function by factoring out the term of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{1-r}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{5 + 2x} &= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (1)/((1 + \\frac{2}{5}x)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot (1)/([1 - (-\\frac{2}{5}x)])\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand the right-hand side using the geometric series formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}[1 + (-\\frac{2}{5}x) + (-\\frac{2}{5}x)^{2} + (-\\frac{2}{5}x)^{3} + ⋯]\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5} + \\frac{3}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5})x + \\frac{3}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5})^{2}x^{2} + \\frac{3}{5}(-\\frac{2}{5})^{3}x^{3} + ⋯\n$$\n\nFinally, we identify that the general term $a_n$ of our sequence is\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{3}{5}\\left(-\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)^n\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73b39364-f9be-54af-a8bd-bc82f6e26643" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5888 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180732 :tutorial/content "In some cases, a generating function can be written as a combination of simpler functions that, in turn, can be further reduced to geometric series.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the generating function\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+6}{2-x-6x^2}\\,\n$$\n\nTo calculate the generating series, we first factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+6}{2-x-6x^2} = \\dfrac{2x+6}{(1-2x)(2+3x)}\n$$\n\nThen, we express the function as a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+6}{(1-2x)(2+3x)} = \\dfrac{2}{1-2x} + \\dfrac{2}{2+3x}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand each of the partial fractions as a geometric series:\n\nFor the first sequence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{1 - 2x} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - 2x} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (1 + (2x) + (2x)^{2} + (2x)^{3}) \\\\\n&= (2) + (2)^{2}x + (2)^{3}x^{2} + (2)^{4}x^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general term of the sequence associated with this generating series is\n\n$$\nb_n=2^{n+1}\n$$\n\nFor the second sequence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2}{2 + 3x} &= \\frac{2}{2} \\cdot (1)/([1 + (\\frac{3}{2})]) \\\\\n&= (1)/([1 + (\\frac{3}{2})]) \\\\\n&= 1 + (-\\frac{3}{2}x) + (-\\frac{3}{2}x)^{2} + (-\\frac{3}{2}x)^{3} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general term of the sequence associated with this generating series is\n\n$$\nc_n=\\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^n\n$$\n\nFinally, the general term of the sequence associated with our original function is\n\n$$\na_n = b_n + c_n = 2^{n+1} + \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^n\\,\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3eabeb0b-16a4-5b9b-b114-1dc823efc313" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5897 :tutorial/title "Determining the General Term of a Sequence Using Partial Fractions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180763 :tutorial/content "Not all vector fields have an associated stream function! So, it's useful to have some way of determining whether a given vector field has a stream function or not. For this, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Let $\\mathbf F= P\\,\\mathbf{i} + Q\\,\\mathbf{j}$ be a vector field defined in a simply connected open set $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2.$ The vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has a stream function $\\psi(x,y)$ if and only if*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y} = 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, the divergence of $\\mathbf F$ must be zero everywhere for a stream function to exist. \n\nWe won't give a detailed proof of this result here, but it's quite easy to see why it may be true. \n\nRecall that if $\\psi(x,y)$ is a stream function of $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ if it satisfies\n\n$$\nP = \\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial y}, \\qquad \nQ = -\\dfrac{\\partial \\psi}{\\partial x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the divergence of this vector field, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂y∂x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y}-\\frac{{∂}^{2}ψ}{∂x∂y} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "61b21c71-3509-5455-86ba-1898b599345d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7744 :tutorial/title "An Existence Condition for Stream Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185300 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a\\neq 0.$ We can derive a general solution to this equation by completing the square.\n\nFirst, let's subtract $c$ from both sides to isolate the variable terms:\n\n$$\nax^2+bx = -c\n$$\n\nSince $a\\neq 0,$ dividing by $a$ gives\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{b}{a}x = -\\dfrac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side and move the constant terms to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{b}{2a}x=-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-{(\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2}-\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}} & =-\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{b}{2a})}^{2} & =\\frac{{b}^{2}}{4{a}^{2}}-\\frac{c}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common factor of $4a^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{b}{2a}\\right)^2  = \\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of both sides yields\n\n$$\nx + \\dfrac{b}{2a}  = \\pm \\sqrt{\\dfrac{b^2 - 4ac}{4a^2}}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{b}{2a} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we isolate $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}.\n$$\n\nThis formula is called the **quadratic formula**. You'll learn more about how to apply the quadratic formula in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "cc56cc55-b423-5e0b-aed0-e07bdb86f874" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13276 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Formula"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185313 :tutorial/content "When we take a power of a trigonometric function, we write the power after the function but before the argument. \n\nFor instance $\\sin^2 30^\\circ$ is the same as $(\\sin30^\\circ)^2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡{30}^{∘} & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘})^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, $\\tan^4 \\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right)$ is the same as $\\left(\\tan \\left(\\dfrac\\pi 3\\right)\\right)^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & ={(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{4} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}\\cdot {(\\sqrt{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "03c172f7-f624-575b-9b19-ecc0979231dd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2726 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185327 :tutorial/content "Let's derive our special case formula from the binomial theorem for positive integer $n\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + {n \\choose 3}{a^{n - 3}}{b^3}  +  \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=1$ and $b=x$ into the above, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x)^{n} & =(\\frac{n}{0})\\cdot {1}^{n}\\cdot {x}^{0}+(\\frac{n}{1})\\cdot {1}^{n-1}\\cdot {x}^{1}+(\\frac{n}{2})\\cdot {1}^{n-2}\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n})\\cdot {1}^{0}\\cdot {x}^{n} \\\\  & =(\\frac{n}{0})+(\\frac{n}{1})x+(\\frac{n}{2}){x}^{2}+(\\frac{n}{3}){x}^{3}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){x}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that\n\n$$\n{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\cdot r!}\n$$\n\nand the particular cases\n\n$$\n{n \\choose 0} = 1, \\qquad {n \\choose 1} = n.\n$$\n\nWe can now continue to simplify our binomial expansion as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+x)^{n} & =(\\frac{n}{0})+(\\frac{n}{1})x+(\\frac{n}{2}){x}^{2}+(\\frac{n}{3}){x}^{3}+⋯+(\\frac{n}{n}){x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+n\\cdot x+\\frac{n!}{(n-2)!\\cdot 2!}\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{n!}{(n-3)!\\cdot 3!}\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+n\\cdot x+\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)!}{(n-2)!\\cdot 2!}\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)(n-3)!}{(n-3)!\\cdot 3!}\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+n\\cdot x+\\frac{n(n-1)\\cancel{(n-2)!}}{\\cancel{(n-2)!}\\cdot 2!}\\cdot {x}^{2}+\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)\\cancel{(n-3)!}}{\\cancel{(n-3)!}\\cdot 3!}\\cdot {x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+nx+\\frac{n(n-1)}{2!}{x}^{2}+\\frac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!}{x}^{3}+⋯+{x}^{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the desired result." :tutorial/id #uuid "557e7ed8-cef1-5e26-8530-47431936b2f7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6036 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Special Case From the General Binomial Theorem"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185342 :tutorial/content "![](images/a0/a0b4ad67ea80f6d6eec3a15b3df18d551d84263c7424405588bd15162ef4e7c2.png)\n\nThe polar curve shown above is defined as\n\n$$\nr(\\theta) = 4 - 3\\cos \\theta, \\qquad 0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe have $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ at the point $P.$ The tangent to the curve at $P$ is shown in the diagram. \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at $P,$ we follow four steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$\n\nUsing techniques that we've previously discussed, we can show that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+3}{0-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(4-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $\\left( 0, 4 \\right).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "febc7b49-9662-5e78-a7a5-cbe9b1f596ba" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6186 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185347 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for sine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin v = \\dfrac{3}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\sin u = -\\dfrac{12}{13}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\sin\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant? \n\nFirst, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{v}$, $\\cos{v}$, and $\\sin{u}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(-\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{12}{13})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{12}^{2}}{{13}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{169}{169}-\\frac{144}{169} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{169}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{169}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the third quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{5}{13}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{12}{13})(\\frac{4}{5})-(-\\frac{5}{13})(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{48}{65}+\\frac{15}{65} \\\\  & =-\\frac{33}{65}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "699a9021-a261-5b19-b767-75fd52b030bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6203 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185350 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for cosine together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nLet's suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac{1}{3}, \\qquad\n\\sin v = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad\n\\cos v = -\\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$ given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u = 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 u = 1- \\sin^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡u & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u= -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\t\t\\cos(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3} \\right) \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right) +  \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right) \\\\[5pt]\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\dfrac{3}{15} \\\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{8\\sqrt{2}+3}{15}\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "75e177a6-4974-5699-b211-c562c0b8bafe" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6209 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185351 :tutorial/content "We can compute trigonometric ratios using the sum and difference formulas for tangent together with the Pythagorean identity.\n\nSuppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3}{5}, \\qquad \\tan v = 3\n$$\n\nHow can we find the value of $\\tan(u+v)$ given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant?\n\nFirst, we recall the sum formula for tangent:\n\n$$\n\\tan(u+v) = \\dfrac{\\tan{u}+\\tan{v}}{1-\\tan{u}\\tan{v}}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the value of $\\tan u$. To do that, we first find the value of $\\sin u$ using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} u &= 1 - \\cos^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{3}{5})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{3^{2}}{5^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{9}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{25}{25} - \\frac{9}{25} \\\\\n&= \\frac{16}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{4}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u = -\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute $\\tan u$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan u &= \\frac{\\sin u}{\\cos u} = ((-\\frac{4}{5}))/((\\frac{3}{5})) =-\\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the sum formula for the tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan (u + v) &= \\frac{\\tan u + \\tan v}{1 - \\tan u\\tan v} \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{4}{3} + 3))/((1 - (-\\frac{4}{3}) \\cdot 3)) \\\\\n&= ((-\\frac{4}{3} + \\frac{9}{3}))/((1 - (-4))) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{5}{3}))/(5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b240b190-952d-5bfa-b552-22de455b671b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6210 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185355 :tutorial/content "Suppose the sequence $a_n$ is defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nand that $f(x)$ is a function such that\n\n- $f(n)=a_n$ for all $n \\geq 1$,\n- $f(x)$ is continuous for $x \\geq 1$, and\n- $f(x)$ is differentiable for $x > 1$.\n\nThen,\n\n- if $f'(x) \\geq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *increasing,* and\n- if $f'(x) \\leq 0$ for $x \\gt 1$, then $a_n$ is *decreasing.*\n\nAs an example, let's show that the following sequence is decreasing:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac1n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac 1x\n$$\n\nfor\n\n$$\nx\\geq 1\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(n)=a_n$ for all $n \\geq 1$,\n- $f(x)$ is continuous for $x \\geq 1$, and\n- $f(x)$ is differentiable for $x > 1$.\n\nTaking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (\\frac{1}{x})^{′} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{x^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f'(x)$ is negative for $x \\gt 1$, we conclude that $a_n$ is a decreasing sequence." :tutorial/id #uuid "2e76e36d-94bc-50f7-b002-87550777e07d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6280 :tutorial/title "Determining Whether a Sequence Is Monotonic Using Derivatives"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185359 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how to deal with limits involving inverse trigonometric functions. \n\nLet's begin by recalling the graph of $y=\\arcsin(x).$\n\n![](images/df/df22eea4331c47da3047381ca672fd80ebf75118054316fe88c0e2d96dae0d34.png)\n\nUsing the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The domain of $\\arcsin(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$\n\n- For every value $a \\in (-1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x) = \\arcsin(a).\n$$\n\n \nIn other words, we can evaluate the limit using direct substitution. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1/2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For any value $a$ that does *not* lie in the domain of $\\arcsin(x),$ the limit\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to a} \\arcsin(x)\n$$\n\n \ndoes *not* exist.\n\n- At the point $x=-1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the left-sided limit does not exist. However, the right-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Similarly, at the point $x=1,$ the limit does *not* exist because the right-sided limit does not exist. However, the left-sided limit *does* exist and is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x) & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit properties of $y=\\arccos(x)$ are similar. Let's discuss them briefly." :tutorial/id #uuid "f1bc1975-006b-5be4-ad5c-88888abb00d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6599 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185360 :tutorial/content "We can deduce limits at infinity of transformed inverse trigonometric functions by sketching their graphs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this limit, we first sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/51/51a0b9a210147b3791ce20a2f65320e20618826671ea6c7c31a27f8647595079.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   Moreover, from the graph, we see that \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x\\to \\infty} \\arctan \\left(x-\\pi\\right)=\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} }  .\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the algebra of limits, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLimits at infinity involving the inverse sine and cosine functions usually do not exist. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "24cdcba9-d6c5-5719-a086-34fe417cc42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6600 :tutorial/title "Limits of Inverse Trigonometric Function at Infinity"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185383 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the formula for factoring a perfect square:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nAs we've seen, **completing the square** on a quadratic expression means expressing it as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to complete the square on a quadratic expression when the coefficient of the linear term is odd. In these cases, we get a fraction in the final expression.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the following quadratic expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(x^2 + 5x + \\left(\\dfrac 52\\right)^2\\right) + 1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the large parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {x}^{2}+5x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}x+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "858a20e8-2e9b-5ab0-ae5f-811c370a9b56" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9421 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186185384 :tutorial/content "Let's complete the square on the following expression: \n\n$$\nx^2 + 3x + 4\n$$\n\nThe procedure is very similar to the one we encountered when the coefficient of the linear term was even.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{4}}$ last:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first three terms now form a perfect square!\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}x  + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}\n$$\n\nFactoring the perfect square, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2  - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{3}{2}}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{4}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+4 \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{16}{4} \\\\\n&= {(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{7}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dba1a2d3-8092-514c-905c-9f6c001081ae" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9422 :tutorial/title "Completing the Square"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189364 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\cos^2 \\theta,$ we obtain our secant-tangent identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\ 1+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2} & ={(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2} \\\\ 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we subtract $1$ from both sides, we isolate the $\\tan^2\\theta$ term.\n\n$$\n\\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta - 1\n$$\n\nThis is an **alternate form** of the secant-tangent identity. \n\nWe can get a second alternate form if we again start with \n\n$$\n1 + \\tan^2\\theta = \\sec^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubtracting $\\tan^2\\theta$ from both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\sec^2\\theta - \\tan^2\\theta = 1.\n$$\n\nWe can use these alternate forms to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d5b1ac1-9a35-55c8-aafd-4634168aa751" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2487 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189374 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7b372073-8035-5855-9367-f29c35485805" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6269 :tutorial/title "Using Limits to Find Vertical Tangents"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189391 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a54c219-b6df-5e73-8673-14f48b1d69af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6384 :tutorial/title "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189408 :tutorial/content "Most statistical computer packages have functionality that allows data from a particular probability distribution to be simulated. In this lesson, we'll learn how this process works.\n\nFor example, suppose we have a random variable with the cumulative distribution function \n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}\\,, \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=F(x)$ is shown below:\n\n![](images/f2/f26b4b7871d1f2b4c7a983986f596c4b599d24fbf34c87b443fb21025f26b5ad.png)\n\n \n\nSuppose we want to generate a sample of size $n=3$ from this distribution. To do this, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, draw a random sample of size $n=3$ from the uniform distribution $Y \\sim U[0,1].$\n\n- Then, find the values $x_i$ such that $y_i=F(x_i).$ In other words, \n\n$$\nx_i=F^{-1}(y_i), \\qquad i=1,2,3.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x)$ is strictly increasing and therefore $F^{-1}(y)$ exists for all $y\\in (0,1).$ \n\nSuppose that we run our simulation and generate $n=3$ numbers from $Y\\sim U[0,1],$ and that our three numbers (rounded to $3$ decimal places) are \n\n$$\ny_1 = 0.214, \\qquad y_2 = 0.707, \\qquad y_3 = 0.819.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe'll now use this data to generate three numbers $x_1, x_2,$ and $x_3$ drawn from the distribution with CDF $F(x).$ The three $x_i$'s are the numbers shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d9/d93db6abed9b1f227e5badb0d71868b1aee261d803af929e1fd8e45dc5f2932b.png)\n\nTo compute our $x_i$'s, we first need to find the inverse of $F(x).$ So we first write $y=F(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+{e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1}{y}-1 \\\\ {e}^{-x} & =\\frac{1-y}{y} \\\\ -x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-y}{y}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{(\\frac{1-y}{y})}^{-1} \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y}{1-y}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 < y < 1.$\n\nFinally, substituting the values $y_1,y_2,y_3$ into the formula for $x=F^{-1}(y),$ we obtain \n\n$$\nx_1 \\approx -1.301, \\qquad x_2 \\approx 0.881, \\qquad x_3 \\approx 1.510.\n$$\n\nThe algorithm above can be formalized in the following theorem:\n\n*Let the random variable $Y \\sim U[0,1]$ be uniformly distributed. Suppose that the function $F(x)$ is a cumulative distribution function, where $F(a) = 0, F(b) = 1,$ and $F$ is strictly monotonic on $a \\lt x \\lt b,$ where $a$ and $b$ could be infinite. Then, the random variable $X,$ given by \n\n$$\nX = F^{-1}(Y)\n$$\n\n is a continuous random variable with the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$*" :tutorial/id #uuid "88037a74-7521-5005-a92c-460e535c9d9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6486 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193843 :tutorial/content "![](images/04/0420b22be0c7c084c39d99e7308e83bb05feb44c1e7ae29222f53e98b0baa200.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\angle{AOB}$ shown above represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. How can we find the measure of this angle?\n\nThe angle $\\angle AOB$ represents one-tenth of a full turn. Since a full turn has a measure of $360^\\circ$, the measure of $\\angle AOB$ is one-tenth of $360^\\circ$.\n\nTherefore, we can express the degree measure of $\\angle{AOB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10} \\cdot 360^{∘} &= \\frac{1 \\cdot 360^{∘}}{10} \\\\ &= \\frac{360^{∘}}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt's common to write the fraction in parentheses and place the degree symbol outside, as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{360}{10})^{∘}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify this fraction by expressing it as a division.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{360}{10}} & \\displaystyle  = \\\\ \\displaystyle 360 \\div  10 &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 36 \\hspace{-0.167em} \\cancel{0} &  \\\\ \\displaystyle 36 & \\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the measure of $\\angle AOB$ is $36^\\circ$." :tutorial/id #uuid "985d6c0a-e42d-5fad-99fa-c411e32b109d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6623 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193854 :tutorial/content "We can evaluate more complex integrals involving arctangent by making use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "76621540-8f9a-5044-ac2e-e03e968de04d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6704 :tutorial/title "A Reminder of Some Useful Results"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193915 :tutorial/content "Now, let's solve an inequality that contains a rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2y-1}{3} \\lt 5\n$$\n\nFirst, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y-1}{3} & <5 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{3}) & <3\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{3}}) & <15 \\\\ 2y-1 & <15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-1 & <15 \\\\ 2y-1+1 & <15+1 \\\\ 2y & <16 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & <\\frac{16}{2} \\\\ y & <8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y <  8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "31078899-8e97-5c79-a990-df51f4a9f773" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8107 :tutorial/title "Clearing Rational Expression in Linear Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193916 :tutorial/content "Finally, let's consider an inequality that contains rational expressions on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+1}{4} \\leq \\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThe rational expressions on both sides of this inequality have positive denominators $4$ and $2.$ The least common multiple of $4$ and $2$ is ${\\color{red}{4}}.$\nThus, we can clear the rational expressions by multiplying both sides of the inequality ${\\color{red}{4}}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{x}{2} \\\\ 4\\cdot (\\frac{x+1}{4}) & \\le 4\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ x+1 & \\le 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & \\le 2x \\\\ x+1-2x & \\le 2x-2x \\\\ 1-x & \\le 0 \\\\ 1-x-1 & \\le 0-1 \\\\ -x & \\le -1 \\\\ \\frac{-x}{(-1)} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\geq 1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5ec24500-fe0f-5068-b9fa-f19ce733798a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8110 :tutorial/title "Clearing Two Rational Expressions in Linear Inequalities"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193942 :tutorial/content "We must be extra careful when converting a unit of area to a larger unit.\n\nTo demonstrate, suppose that a square has an area of $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$. Let's use unit arguments to express this area in square centimeters.\n\nWe start by recalling the conversion table for units of length.\n\n| Name | Symbol | Conversion |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Kilometer | $\\textrm{km}$ | $1\\,\\textrm{km} = 1\\,000\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Meter | $\\textrm{m}$ | Base Unit |\n| Centimeter | $\\textrm{cm}$ | $100\\,\\textrm{cm} = 1\\,\\textrm{m}$ |\n| Millimeter | $\\textrm{mm}$ | $10\\,\\textrm{mm} = 1\\,\\textrm{cm}$ |\n\nSince we're converting from $\\textrm{mm}^2$ to $\\textrm{cm}^2$, we start with the conversion between $\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{cm}$. According to our table, there are $10\\,\\textrm{mm}$ per $\\textrm{cm}$. We can write this ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{10\\,\\textrm{mm}}{\\textrm{cm}}\n$$\n\nThe problem with using this conversion factor is that it does not contain area units. However, we can convert this into area units by squaring it and applying the rules of exponents as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{10mm}{cm})^{2} &= \\frac{10^{2}mm^{2}}{cm^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100mm^{2}}{cm^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since we're converting from a smaller unit of area $(\\textrm{mm}^2)$ to a larger one $(\\textrm{cm}^2)$, we need to multiply $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ by the *reciprocal* of the above, namely\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{cm}^2}{100\\,\\textrm{mm}^2}\n$$\n\nThis is our conversion factor.\n\nNow, multiplying $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ by our conversion factor, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{mm}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100 \\hspace{0.167em} \\cancel{ {\\text{mm}}^{2}}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{cm}}^{2}}{100}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{7 \\hspace{0.167em} 200}{100}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 72 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\text{cm}}^{2} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $7\\,200 \\, \\textrm{mm}^2$ is equivalent to $72 \\,\\textrm{cm}^2$." :tutorial/id #uuid "e5064f72-271f-5912-8df7-8be286bc3d45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199468 :tutorial/content "Remember that when we measure the flux of a vector field through a *closed* surface, we always calculate with respect to an outward-pointing unit normal vector.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the vector field $\\mathbf F,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\,0, \\, -y^2 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the conical solid shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0be83ceada68d0b56be4872f21652a7d18932ff059ea704576ca3cce892fa87b.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$ \n\nFirst, since $S$ is a closed surface, we have that\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 2 - \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}$ is oriented *upward*, and\n\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = 0$ is oriented *downward*.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- First, we consider the upper cone $S_1.$ \n\nWe compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})=-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle,$ then\n\n$$\nP = x, \\qquad\nQ = 0, \\qquad\nR = -y^2.\n$$\n\n \nAs a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-x\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})-0\\cdot (-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}})+(-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\n- Next, we consider the lower plane $S_2.$ \n\nSince the positive orientation is downward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,-1 \\rangle.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨x,0,-{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0,-1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the entire surface is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A+{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73467c61-810e-5e81-abcc-b5eb99d1e0ab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7651 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199471 :tutorial/content "Repeated integrals can feature three or more variables. In this lesson, we'll learn how to evaluate repeated integrals containing three variables.\n\nFor example, let's consider the repeated integral below:\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $(z),$ then with respect to the middle variable $(y),$ and finally with respect to the outer variable $(x).$ \n\nWe can write the first step more explicitly as\n\n$$\n%\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x=\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\right] \\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x.\n%= \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2 \\left[ \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z \\right] \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration.\nFirst, we integrate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $z,$ *treating $x$ and $y$ as constants:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}x(y+z)^{2}\\,\\text{d}z]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{[\\frac{(y+z)^{3}}{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x(\\frac{(y+1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe next step is to integrate with respect to $y,$ which we can write more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13 \\int_{1}^2 \\left[ \\int_0^2x \\left( (y+1)^3 - y^3 \\right) \\textrm{d}y \\right] \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the inner integral by integrating with respect to $y,$ *treating $x$ as a constant,* we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x((y+1)^{3}-{y}^{3})\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x{[(\\frac{(y+1)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x[(\\frac{81}{4}-\\frac{16}{4})-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}x\\cdot (\\frac{64}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}16x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{[8{x}^{2}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(32-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_{1}^2 \\int_0^2 \\int_0^1 x(y+z)^2 \\:\\textrm{d}z\\:\\textrm{d}y\\:\\textrm{d}x = 8.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b3640bfe-4972-5ef4-bbf6-4c5a272dd506" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7655 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199475 :tutorial/content "![](images/06/06abbcf6887f4b36ad85e4623a5c439f3613fdf7c2f3b342df2ff54fecbc86f6.png)\n\n  \n\nOur goal is to find the image of the region $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nLet's first find the images of the horizontal sides:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$ Substituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents a line segment that lies on a line passing through the origin. The slope of this line must be\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{\\sin \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)}{\\cos \\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg)} = \\tan\\bigg(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\bigg),\n$$\n\nmeaning that the line makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. \nThe endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca644fa90870697e9cea1740c6432d43f682c650521217f19ea6f87c09cba0fd.png)\n\n- Similarly, we can find the image of the top side, where $v=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{5}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 2.$\n\n![](images/11/118c9d18e4d8158014643771c9fa018b1b15da38184f693b89d07f9fadd3c748.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8be5595b-5342-5f9e-9e69-9591c32d7780" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7670 :tutorial/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199500 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find the points of intersection of the elliptic paraboloid \n$z=x^2+y^2$\nand the line with the equation\n$x = -y = -z+1.$\nThe line, paraboloid, and their points of intersection are shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5159ac99a1bc9f73c01df3c2cc2e6fb2093a3d68e00731609d7b62a6fa55149d.png)\n\n \n\nThe trick is to express the equation of the line in parametric form, substitute the parametric equations into the equation of the surface, and solve for the parameter.\n\nSo, first, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n![](images/01/01357a5c6b72d7c976765aeed04db8afa3e3a80e13095d9be1ea2f2594ae5bf5.svg)\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nOur intersection points must belong to the line and the surface simultaneously. So, substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (-t+1) & =(t)^{2}+(-t)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying this equation, we get\n\n$$\n2t^2+t - 1=0.\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are\n$t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $t=-1.$ \n\nSubstituting these values back into the parametric equations of the line\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y=-(\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z=-(\\frac{1}{2})+1=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2}).$\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=-(-1)=1 \\\\ z=-(-1)+1=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(-1,1,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{1}{2},\\dfrac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,1,2).$ \n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "90b0a614-08e3-5d8b-bcf4-28aacc99e1ee" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199504 :tutorial/content "Here, we derive the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form.\n\nSuppose that the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by\n\n![](images/df/dfa82e503eeaa38943edddc7d6982cf4893c73fd2f6d075dd390f51cd0450091.svg)\n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a}.$\nBy restricting $\\mathbf x$ to a line through $\\mathbf{a},$ we can consider $f$ as a function of one variable. Then, we'll use the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function to derive the formula.\n\nLet $\\mathbf{x}_0$ be a constant vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ Then, the line through $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = \\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}), \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{x}(1) = \\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nNow, we define a function $F: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by restricting the domain of $f$ to this line:\n\n$$\nF(t)\n= f(\\mathbf{x}(t))\n%= f(\\mathbf{a} + t(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a}))\n$$\n\nThe second-degree Taylor polynomial of this single-variable function about $t=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(t) \\approx F(0) + F'(0)t + \\dfrac12F''(0)t^2.\n$$\n\nWe can find the first derivative of $F$ using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(a+t({x}_{0}-a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}\\mathbf{x}}$ is a row vector and $(\\mathbf{x}_0 - \\mathbf{a})$ is a column vector, this is a scalar quantity and, hence, equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({x}_{0}-a))}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith this, we can find the second derivative of $F{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}F}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\text{d}F}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}) \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =({x}_{0}-a)^{T}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}f}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}_{0}-a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first and second derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) &={f}^{′}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a) \\\\ {F}^{″}(t) &=({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(x(t))({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function and its derivatives at $t=0$ and using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}(0) = \\mathbf{a},$ we have\n\n![](images/ba/ba4825ec7e9bb46e08df09c8b46b941c36a69ffd6e72081d09cd1ef6134a54d8.svg)\n\nPutting these values into the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a single-variable function about $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & ≈F(0)+{F}^{′}(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}{F}^{″}(0){t}^{2} \\\\  & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)t+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a){t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using this polynomial to approximate the value of $F(1),$ and the fact that $F(1) = f(\\mathbf{x}(1)) = f(\\mathbf{x}_0),$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a)\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\ f({x}_{0}) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)({x}_{0}-a)+\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{0}-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)({x}_{0}-a).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\mathbf{x}_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is an arbitrary constant. Therefore, we can relax the assumption that $\\mathbf{x}_0$ is fixed and replace it with a general point $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ near $\\mathbf{a}$ to obtain the formula for the second-degree Taylor polynomial of a multivariable function in vector form:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a}) + \\dfrac{1}{2} (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})^T \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a}) (\\mathbf{x} - \\mathbf{a})\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de526622-dda2-5168-9ec1-6b123e04fa54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7862 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199548 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $a_n$ is a sequence defined for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 1\n$$\n\nWe know that the *limit* of a sequence (if it exists) is the unique number $L$ such that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = L\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this statement means that the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want, provided that $n$ is large enough.\n\nWhile intuition is important, none of this is very precise. What does \"the distance between $a_n$ and $L$ can be as small as we want\" mean? And what about \"provided that $n$ is large enough\"? Can we quantify these ideas more precisely?\n\nWe need a precise mathematical definition of what's meant by *the limit of a sequence.* This lesson aims to construct a suitable definition in the case where $L=0$.\n\nWe start with a definition.\n\n> *A **null sequence** is a sequence that converges to zero.*\n\nFor example, the sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{1+n^2}, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)a_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{1}{1 + n^{2}}) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the sequence\n\n$$\nb_n = n+1, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nis *not* a null sequence since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)b_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)(n + 1) \\\\\n&= ∞ \\\\\n&\\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d7006fb2-b7a0-568a-952a-8b238bc5cb7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8179 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199563 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = 2\\sqrt{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence is divergent:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\left(2\\sqrt n\\right) = \\infty\n$$\n\nGiven that $M > 0$, let's find a natural number $N(M)$ such that for all\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na_n > M\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the inequality $a_n > M$ for $n{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{n} & > M \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & > \\frac{M}{2} \\\\ n & > (\\frac{M}{2})^{2} \\\\ n & > \\frac{M^{2}}{4}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$\n\nAnd that's it! Given any $M >0$, no matter how large, $a_n$ is guaranteed to be larger than $M$ provided that\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the smallest natural number larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{M^2}{4}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9557af99-f4d0-5b15-8ef6-26c268aca9c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8246 :tutorial/title "Finding a Suitable N"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199596 :tutorial/content "In previous lessons, we saw how to define the limit of a null sequence (i.e., a sequence whose limit equals zero) and how to construct a formal proof that a given sequence is null.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll extend our understanding to include sequences that converge a finite (not necessarily zero) limit. If such a limit exists, it's usually denoted as $L$.\n\nConsider the following sequence:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{n+1}{n}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that the limit of this sequence is\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}}={\\color{red}{1}}{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(n → ∞)a_{n} &= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{n + 1}{n}) \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(\\frac{n}{n} + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= lim_(n → ∞)(1 + \\frac{1}{n}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 0 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N(\\varepsilon)$ such that the distance between $a_n$ and\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{L}} = {\\color{red}{1}}\n$$\n\nis *smaller than* $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\forall n\\geq N,\\qquad \\mid a_n - {\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nLet's draw a few sketches to illustrate this idea.\n\n- If we select $\\varepsilon = 0.5$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 3$.\n\n![](images/be/bee8ffa92b56e2e8dedc214322481b13a3ab8cd4b73d84a5c1300d10dbd43d4f.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.3$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 4$.\n\n![](images/d2/d27e06c19457ab1c89386cde995c043c6d6d55169ec8fdc32509690bf1880767.png)\n\n- If $\\varepsilon = 0.1$, then $\\mid a_n-{\\color{red}{1}} \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\geq 11$.\n\n![](images/56/564c5ddf12f0171c5d55da0d669f9f7bff2726707c72e473fa9e8eb6704bd937.png)\n\nThe key idea is this: By making $\\varepsilon$ smaller and smaller, we can *always* find an index $N$ where the distance between $a_n$ and $\\color{red}L$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "01b59a18-c546-5f7f-9fd3-34c58eb29400" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8387 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199605 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the null geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that for $a_n$ is larger than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4$ when $n={\\color{red}{19}}$. Indeed,\n$a_{\\color{red}{19}} = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^{\\color{red}{19}} \\approx 0.000\\,451 > \\varepsilon$.\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}$, we have that $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,4{:}$\n$a_{20}|= (\\frac{2}{3})^{20} \\approx 0.000301 < ε✓; a_{21}|= (\\frac{2}{3})^{21} \\approx 0.000200 < ε✓; a_{22}|= (\\frac{2}{3})^{22} \\approx 0.000134 < ε✓; a_{23}|= (\\frac{2}{3})^{23} \\approx 0.000089 < ε✓; ⋮$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $\\varepsilon$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,1\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{23}}\n$$\n\nIndeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{22} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{22} \\approx 0.000134 > ε\\cdot \\\\\na_{23} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{23} \\approx 0.000089 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{24} \\approx 0.000059 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= (\\frac{2}{3})^{25} \\approx 0.000040 < ε✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a null sequence is that *no matter how small* $\\varepsilon$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $\\varepsilon$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $\\varepsilon$, i.e.,\n\n$$\nN= N(\\varepsilon)\n$$\n\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence is null relies on finding $N(\\varepsilon)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "edfa3100-6ba9-5da0-981b-6923da7603e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9216 :tutorial/title "Further Discussion of a Prior Result"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199615 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence diverges to infinity since it's a positive geometric sequence with common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac97 > 1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n = \\infty\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\nM = 8\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that $a_n$ for $n={\\color{red}{63}}$ is smaller than $M = 8\\,000\\,000$. Indeed,\n$a_{\\color{red}{63}} = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^{\\color{red}{63}} \\approx 7\\,518\\,000 < M$.\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}$, we have that $a_n$ is larger than $M= 8\\,000\\,000{:}$\n$a_{64}|= (\\frac{9}{7})^{64} \\approx 9666000 > M✓; a_{65}|= (\\frac{9}{7})^{65} \\approx 12428000 > M✓; a_{66}|= (\\frac{9}{7})^{66} \\approx 15978000 > M✓; ⋮$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $M$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\nM = 15\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{66}}\n$$\n\nIndeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{65} &= (\\frac{9}{7})^{65} \\approx 12428000 < M\\cdot \\\\\na_{66} &= (\\frac{9}{7})^{66} \\approx 15978000 > M✓ \\\\\na_{67} &= (\\frac{9}{7})^{67} \\approx 20544000 > M✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a sequence with a (positive) infinite limit is that *no matter how large* $M$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $M$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $M$, i.e.,\n$N= N(M)$.\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence diverges to infinity relies on finding $N(M)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "62a5f0d1-ba0d-5e4b-b83d-83a3bb42f612" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9232 :tutorial/title "Further Discussion of a Prior Result"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205176 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will learn how the hypergeometric distribution can be used to model real-world situations. We'll also discuss the structure of a hypergeometric random variable's probability mass function (PMF).\n\nSuppose a chess club has $18$ members, of whom $12$ are veteran players and $6$ are beginners. A sample of $6$ players is randomly chosen to participate in a chess tournament. If $X$ represents the number of veterans in the sample, find an expression that represents\n\n$$\nP(X = x)\n$$\n\nfor $X \\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$.\n\nThe probability that the sample contains precisely $x$ veteran players is given by\n$$\nP(X = x) = \\dfrac{ {\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ with } {x} \\text{ successes}}}{ {\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ }}}.\n$$\n\nHere, a \"success\" means selecting a veteran player, and a \"failure\" means choosing a beginner.\n\nLet's calculate the numerator and denominator separately.\n\n- First, let's find the number of samples of size $6$ with precisely $x$ veteran players. The number of ways of selecting $x$ veteran players from a total of $12$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{12}C_{x} = \\binom{12}{x}$.\nThe other $6-x$ players in the sample are beginners. The number of ways of selecting $6-x$ beginners from a total of $18-12=6$ in the population is\n$\\displaystyle {}_{6}C_{6-x} = \\binom{6}{6-x}$.\nSo, by the rule of product, the number of samples of size $6$ with precisely $x$ veteran players is\n$# of samples with [math]x[/math] veteran players|= # of ways to select [math]x[/math] veteran players; \\cdot # of ways to select [math](6-x)[/math] beginners; = (\\frac{12}{x}) \\cdot (\\frac{6}{6 - x})$.\n- Next, we find the number of samples of size $6$ from a population of size $18$. This is given by\n${}_{18}C_{6} = \\binom{18}{6}$.\n\nTherefore, for $X \\in \\{0,1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = x ) & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{# of samples of size } {6} \\text{ with } {x} \\text{ successes}}{\\text{# of samples of size } {6}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{12}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}x}}} \\right) \\cdot  \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}6}}}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}6 - x}}} \\right)}}{ {\\displaystyle \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}18}}}{6} \\right)}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7cc148-69d0-50a7-a2a9-1084e9317512" :tutorial/math-academy-id 11563 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205224 :tutorial/content "We can use proof by contradiction to prove that certain numbers are irrational.\n\nFor example, let's show that $\\sqrt{2}$ is an irrational number. We begin by stating the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot} \\:\\: P: \\quad { {\\sqrt{2}} \\textrm{ is irrational}}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad { {\\sqrt{2}} \\textrm{ is rational}}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.*\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> *Then,*\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> *for some integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n\nThe contradiction we seek is that $a$ and $b$ are, in fact, *not* coprime under this assumption.\n\nWe start by squaring both sides of the equation:\n\n> *So, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt{2})^{2}|= (\\frac{a}{b})^{2}; 2|= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}}; 2b^{2}|= a^{2}$,\n> *which, by the definition of divisibility, means that $2 \\mid a^2$.*\n\nNow, recall the following property of divisibility by a prime $p{:}$\n$p \\mid xy \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (p \\mid x) \\lor (p \\mid y)$\nApplying this property with $p=2$ and $x=y=a$, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\mid a\\cdot a = a^2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (2 \\mid a) \\lor (2 \\mid a)\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $2 \\mid a$.\n\n> *Since $2$ is prime, we have $2 \\mid a$, which means $a = 2c$ where $c$ is an integer.*\n\nNow, we substitute $a=2c$ into the equation $2b^2 = a^2$ and show that $2$ is *also* a factor of $b$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $2b^{2}|= (2c)^{2}; 2b^{2}|= 4c^{2}; b^{2}|= 2c^{2}$\n> *Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $2 \\mid b^2$, which means that $2 \\mid b$.*\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $2$, their greatest common divisor is at least $2$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> *Combining the results $2 \\mid a$ and $2 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 2$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof." :tutorial/id #uuid "dbce2ed2-da3e-586b-b9d9-74768df82d94" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8368 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205276 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *$\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational. Then,*\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> *for some integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n> *So, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{2}|= \\frac{a}{b}; (\\sqrt{2})^{2}|= (\\frac{a}{b})^{2}; 2|= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}}; 2b^{2}|= a^{2}$,\n> *which means that $2 \\mid a^2$.*\n> *Since $2$ is prime, we have $2 \\mid a$, which means $a = 2c$ where $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore,*\n> $2b^{2}|= (2c)^{2}; 2b^{2}|= 4c^{2}; b^{2}|= 2c^{2}$\n> *Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $2 \\mid b^2$, which means that $2 \\mid b$.*\n> *Combining the results $2 \\mid a$ and $2 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 2$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n> *Therefore, $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "0a824b44-1d5c-5e86-86fb-e426eb33a038" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9353 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Proof"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186205283 :tutorial/content "We can use a similar argument to show that a sum or difference of radicals is irrational.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to show that the following expression is irrational:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 3 - \\sqrt 2\n$$\n\nWe assume for a contradiction that this is *rational.*\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 3 - \\sqrt 2 = \\dfrac{a}{b}\n$$\n\nTo isolate a single radical on one side, we first square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{3} - \\sqrt{2})^{2} &= (\\frac{a}{b})^{2} \\\\\n(\\sqrt{3})^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} + (\\sqrt{2})^{2} &= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}} \\\\\n3 - 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{3 \\cdot 2} + 2 &= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}} \\\\\n5 - 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{6} &= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we reduced the original equation containing the radicals $\\sqrt 2$ and $\\sqrt 3$ to an equation containing the single radical\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2\\cdot 3} = \\sqrt 6\n$$\n\nIsolating the radical, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2 \\cdot \\sqrt{6} &= \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}} - 5 \\\\\n2 \\cdot \\sqrt{6} &= 5 - \\frac{a^{2}}{b^{2}} \\\\\n\\sqrt{6} &= \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{a^{2}}{2b^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the terms on the right-hand side over a common denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sqrt{6} &= \\frac{5b^{2}}{2b^{2}} - \\frac{a^{2}}{2b^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5b^{2} - a^{2}}{2b^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $a$ and $b$ are integers, it follows that\n\n$$\nc = 5b^2-a^2\n$$\n\nand $d = 2b^2$ are integers. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\sqrt 6 = \\dfrac{c}{d}\n$$\n\nNow, it can be shown that $\\sqrt 6$ is irrational. So, we have reached a contradiction! Therefore, we conclude that $\\sqrt 3 - \\sqrt 2$ is irrational." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea3aaea1-8593-5303-bc55-30e838f13f38" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9369 :tutorial/title "Proving Irrationality of Sums and Differences of Radicals"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186209877 :tutorial/content "Now, let $\\mathbf{y}: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}^q$ and $f: \\mathbb{R}^q \\to \\mathbb{R}$. Suppose we want to compute the gradient \n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f(\\mathbf{y}(\\mathbf{x})).\n$$\n\nTo do this, we need to apply the chain rule. The chain rule for total derivatives tells us that\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{1 \\times n}\n= \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}}_{1 \\times q}\n\\cdot \n\\underbrace{\\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}}}_{q \\times n}.\n$$\n\nNow, recall that the gradient of a scalar function is the transpose of the total derivative:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f}_{n \\times 1} = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} f}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n$$\n\nTo derive the chain rule in terms of gradients, we apply the identity $(AB)^T = B^T A^T$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}f & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}}_{n\\times q}\\cdot \\underbrace{{(\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}}_{q\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient chain rule is\n\n$$\n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{x}} f = \n\\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{y}}{\\textrm{d} \\mathbf{x}} \\right)^T\n\\cdot \n\\nabla_{\\mathbf{y}} f.\n$$\n\nNote that the order matters; when expressed in the gradient form, the derivatives are applied *from right to left!*" :tutorial/id #uuid "eb90df7d-906c-5d50-a61a-40222511f26e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 12301 :tutorial/title "Computing Gradients With the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214229 :tutorial/content "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$5y|= 32; \\frac{5y}{5}|= \\frac{32}{5}; y|= \\frac{32}{5}$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$5y - 6|= 5 \\cdot (\\frac{32}{5}) - 6; = 32 - 6; = 26$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$5y - 6|= (32) - 6; = 26$\n\nLet's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "07f519ef-09b2-5571-852e-7bf636064c2d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13448 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186214278 :tutorial/content "Suppose the function\n\n$$\nD(t) = 1,250 \\cdot (1.05)^{(3/4)t}\n$$\n\nmodels the value, in thousands of dollars, of an investment $t$ years after it is made.\n\nNote that some exponential models adjust the measurement of time by including a rescaling coefficient in the exponent, such as $3/4$ in this example.\n\nThe investors ask their investment manager the following question:\n\n> *By what percentage is the investment predicted to increase by every $4$ months?*\n\nLet's start by rewriting this model in terms of months instead of years.\n\n- Since there are $12$ months in a year, we substitute $t=\\dfrac{m}{12}$ into the original equation.\n- We *divide* by $12$ (not multiply) because we want $m=12$ months to correspond to $t=1$ year.\n\nSubstituting, we get a model $d(m)$ for the value of the investment as a function of time in months $m$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd(m) = D(\\frac{m}{12}) &= 1, 250 \\cdot (1.05)^{(3/4) \\cdot (m/12)} \\\\\n&= 1, 250 \\cdot (1.05)^{m/16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to find the percentage increase over $4$ months. Inspecting the model in terms of months $d(m)$, the growth factor over $m$ months is\n$(1.05)^{m/16}$.\n\nSo, the growth factor over $4$ months is\n\n$$\n(1.05)^{4/16} = (1.05)^{1/4} \\approx 1.012272\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to an increase of approximately\n\n$$\n1.012272 - 1 = 0.012272 \\approx 1.23\\%\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the investment is predicted to increase by $1.23\\%$ every $4$ months." :tutorial/id #uuid "52347ebd-4cdf-593a-970c-f7d0d4788478" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13585 :tutorial/title "Interpreting Exponential Models Using Different Time Units"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218240 :tutorial/content "In some cases, we might have to deal with a non-linear equation (e.g., an equation containing variable radicals or exponents) with more than one variable. A common task is to isolate one of the variables.\n\nFor example, the equation below relates the distinct positive real numbers $a$, $b$, and $x{:}$\n\n$$\n12\\sqrt{15 - x} = \\dfrac{b}{10a}\n$$\n\nHow can we expresses $x$ in terms of $a$ and $b$?\n\nExpressing $x$ in terms of $a$ and $b$ means putting $x$ on one side of the equation (on its own) and everything else on the other. In this case, isolating $x$ means solving a radical equation containing multiple variables.\n\nSo, to express our equation in terms of $x$, we first isolate the square root by dividing both sides by $12{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 12 \\sqrt{15 - x} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{10 a}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{12 \\sqrt{15 - x}}{12}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{10 a \\cdot  12}} \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{12} \\sqrt{15 - x}}{\\cancel{12}}} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{10 a \\cdot  12}} \\\\ \\displaystyle \\sqrt{15 - x} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{120 a}}\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNext, we square both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\sqrt{15 - x})^{2} &= (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2} \\\\\n15 - x &= (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n15 - x &= (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2} \\\\\n15 - x - 15 &= (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2} - 15 \\\\\n- x &= (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2} - 15 \\\\\nx &= 15 - (\\frac{b}{120a})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx = 15 - \\left(\\dfrac{b}{120a}\\right)^2\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5bd4da99-f392-5483-a6a1-29d181b061fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13747 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218265 :tutorial/content "We need to be more careful when finding the maximum or minimum of a difference or a quotient!\n\nFor example, suppose we have two numbers $a$ and $b$ such that\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 12\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the largest possible value of $a - b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **maximize** the difference of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **largest possible** value of $a$ and subtract the **smallest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nTake a moment to appreciate why this must be true. If we're trying to maximize a difference, we want to start with the largest number possible and then subtract as little as possible from it.\n\nSo, let's compute the largest possible value of $a$, the smallest possible value of $b$, and find their difference:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 12$, the largest possible value of $a$ is $12$.\n$\\max(a) = 12$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the smallest possible value of $b$ is $4$.\n$\\min(b) = 4$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(a - b) &= max(a) - min(b) \\\\\n&= 12 - 4 \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for maximizing a *quotient* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **maximize** the quotient of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **largest possible** value of $a$ and divide by the **smallest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true. We want the dividend to be as large as possible, while the divisor should be as small as possible.\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmax(\\frac{a}{b}) &= (max(a))/(min(b)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{12}{4} \\\\\n&= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f9444e1f-e03b-5ad8-9111-89a19a16fe6f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13781 :tutorial/title "Maximizing Differences and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218295 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our intervals once more.\n\n$$\n1\\leq a \\leq 12\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad 4\\leq b \\leq 8\n$$\n\nWhat is the smallest possible value of $a - b$?\n\nWe can answer this question by applying the following principle:\n\n> *To **minimize** the difference of two numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **smallest possible** value of $a$ and subtract the **largest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true. If we're trying to minimize a difference, we want to start with the smallest number possible and then subtract as much as possible from it.\n\nSo, let's compute the smallest possible value of $a$, the largest possible value of $b$, and find their difference:\n\n- Since $1\\leq a \\leq 12$, the smallest possible value of $a$ is $1$.\n$\\min(a) = 1$\n- Since $4\\leq b \\leq 8$, the largest possible value of $b$ is $8$.\n$\\max(b) = 8$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(a - b) &= min(a) - max(b) \\\\\n&= 1 - 8 \\\\\n&=-7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rule for minimizing a *quotient* is similar, though it only works for positive numbers:\n\n> *To **minimize** the quotient of two **positive** numbers $a$ and $b$, we take the **smallest possible** value of $a$ and divide by the **largest possible** value of $b$.*\n\nAgain, take a moment to appreciate why this must be true.\n\nSo, in this example, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(\\frac{a}{b}) &= (min(a))/(max(b)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8} \\\\\n&= 0.125\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0924a5a-26c8-553e-af55-7958a960fd70" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13834 :tutorial/title "Minimizing Differences and Quotients"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218303 :tutorial/content "When working with exponential functions, we may sometimes restrict the domain to sets other than the usual $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and\n\n$$\nx\\geq 0\n$$\n\nFor instance, consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=7\\left(\\dfrac15\\right)^x+2\n$$\n\non the restricted domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq -1\n$$\n\nWhat extreme values, if any, does $f$ have?\n\nLet's start by sketching the graph of $y=f(x)$ with the domain restriction\n\n$$\nx\\geq -1\n$$\n\n![](images/c0/c02943707c58d8b09894d5039211bd8e862ba2dcb062dfb59ebf762ea9f434f1.png)\n\nNote that we have ignored all values of $f(x)$ for $x < -1$ to accommodate the domain restriction.\n\nFrom the graph, we see that, on the domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq -1\n$$\n\n- $f$ attains its *maximum* at $x=-1$, and\n- $f$ does not have a minimum value, because it approaches (but never reaches) $y=2$ from above as $x\\to\\infty$.\n\nEvaluating $f$ at $x=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-1) &= 7(\\frac{1}{5})^{-1} + 2 \\\\\n&= 7 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\\\\n&= 35 + 2 \\\\\n&= 37\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the domain\n\n$$\nx \\geq -1\n$$\n\n- the maximum value of $f$ is $37$, and\n- the range of the function is given by\n$f\\in (-2, 37]$." :tutorial/id #uuid "54b33cdf-9d61-55d8-9331-841a9f9a012d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 13842 :tutorial/title "Extrema of Exponential Functions Over Other Restricted Domains"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186218364 :tutorial/content "Recall the original equation in terms of the parameter $p{:}$\n\n$$\ny = xp + p^2\n$$\n\nAnd we have the condition for the singular solution:\n\n$$\nx = -2p\n$$\n\nWe now proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 2**: Find the singular solution in parametric form.\n\nLet's substitute this expression for $x$ into the equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the singular solution in parametric form, using $p$ as the parameter:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2p \\\\ y &=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Convert the parametric solution to an explicit function $y(x).$\n\nTo eliminate the parameter $p,$ we solve the first equation for $p{:}$\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny = -\\dfrac{x^2}{4}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7128c704-3b1b-564b-911b-55cd0eaf6dda" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14868 :tutorial/title "Finding the Singular Solution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222498 :tutorial/content "Each step of a numerical method produces a local truncation error $\\tau,$ which, over multiple steps, accumulates into a global truncation error $e.$ Both LTE and GTE depend on step size $\\Delta x.$\n\nThe **order** of a numerical method describes how fast these errors shrink as $\\Delta x$ decreases.\nA numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the *largest positive* integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^p\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0.\n$$\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately $k^p{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(\\frac{Δx}{k}) & ≈K{(\\frac{Δx}{k})}^{p}=\\frac{K(Δx)^{p}}{{k}^{p}}≈\\frac{e(Δx)}{{k}^{p}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate with an example. Suppose a numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.21,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.21) = 0.785.$\n\n- With step size $\\Delta x = 0.07,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.07) = 0.010.$\n\nUsing this data, we can estimate the order of the numerical method.\n\nThe step size decreases from $0.21$ to $0.07.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{0.21}{0.07} = 3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\nk^p = 3^p.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.785$ to $0.010.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0.785}{0.010} = 78.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈78.5 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(78.5) \\\\ p & ≈3.97….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $4.$\n\nBut why is order important? Well, a higher order means that the error decreases faster as $\\Delta x$ decreases. As a result, higher-order methods can achieve a target accuracy *with fewer steps*. In this way, order predicts the accuracy you gain when you make steps smaller." :tutorial/id #uuid "40e84a8b-32df-5566-99ff-2b37f338d69f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14500 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222510 :tutorial/content "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 4x - 2y, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nLet's begin the process of applying the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method to approximate the value of $y(1).$\n\nWe know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1).$ Since the method takes $2$ steps (one to initialize, one to approximate), we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1-0}2 = \\dfrac12.\n$$\n\nWe will use a table to keep track of known values. First, we add the initial condition data:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we initialize the ABM $2$-step method by making a preliminary step with a one-step method.\n\nApplying the RK4 method with step size $\\Delta x = \\dfrac12,$ we obtain the slope $y'=-2,$ increment $\\Delta y = -\\dfrac14,$ and approximation\n\n$$\ny\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)\\approx 1 + \\left(-\\dfrac14\\right) = \\dfrac34.\n$$\n\nWe add the slope to the table, and the approximation in a new row.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ |  |  |  |\n\nNote that, since RK4 is not a predictor-corrector method, there is no increment $\\Delta y_p$ or predictor $y_p$ for this step. So, we leave these entries in the first row blank.\n\nNow, we continue from this point, using the ABM $2$-step method.\nFirst, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =4x-2y \\\\  & =4\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $(x_{-1}, y_{-1}) = \\left(0, 1\\right)$ is $y'_{-1} = -2.$\n\nNext, we compute $\\Delta y_p$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{'}-{y}_{-1}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})-(-2))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,y'\\,$ | $\\Delta y_p$ | $y_p$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac34$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac78$ | $\\dfrac{13}8$ |\n\nTherefore, our initial prediction for $y(1)$ is $y_p = \\dfrac{13}8.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c403981-071d-5b27-8ff8-99c1ea98715b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14615 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222512 :tutorial/content "We'll now show that the definition of order with respect to LTE is the same as with respect to GTE.\n\nIndeed, suppose a numerical method with increment formula $\\Delta y = \\Phi(x, y, \\Delta x) \\cdot \\Delta x$ satisfies the following:\n\n$$\n\\tau(\\Delta x) = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)\\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad \\Delta x \\to 0\n$$\n\nOn a fixed interval of length $L,$ the number of steps is $N \\approx \\dfrac{L}{\\Delta x}.$ So,\n$N = \\mathcal O\\!\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\Delta x}\\right)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$\n\nNow, recall the relationship between GTE and LTE, given by\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N = e_{N-1} + \\left[ \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\right] \\cdot \\Delta x + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nSince $y(x_{N-1}) - y_{N-1} = e_{N-1},$ it is natural to say that changing the input $y$ by $e_{N-1}$ changes $\\Phi$ by about a constant multiple of $e_{N-1}.$ So, heuristically,\n\n$$\n\\Phi(x_{N-1}, y(x_{N-1}), \\Delta x) - \\Phi(x_{N-1}, y_{N-1}, \\Delta x) \\approx Ce_{N-1}\n$$\n\nfor some $C$ that *doesn't* depend on $\\Delta x$ (on a fixed interval). Hence, the GTE satisfies approximately\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) = e_N \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)e_{N-1} + \\tau_N.\n$$\n\nAfter $N$ steps, we get\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx (1+C\\Delta x)^N e_0 + \\sum_{n=1}^N(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}\\tau_n.\n$$\n\nNext, note that $(1+C\\Delta x)^N \\approx e^{CN\\Delta x} \\approx e^{CL},$ which is $\\mathcal O(1)$ as $\\Delta x \\to 0.$ So, the weights $(1+C\\Delta x)^{N-n}$ don't change the *power* of $\\Delta x.$\n\nSo, we can treat the GTE as roughly the sum of the per-step local errors. Therefore,\n\n$$\ne(\\Delta x) \\approx \\sum_{n=1}^N \\tau_n\n$$\n\nWith $\\tau_n = \\mathcal O\\!\\left((\\Delta x)^{p+1}\\right)$ for each $n=1,\\ldots,N,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(Δx) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =N\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,(\\frac{1}{Δx})\\cdot O\\,((Δx)^{p+1}) \\\\  & =O\\,((Δx)^{p}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $p$ is maximal in the LTE definition, this shows the GTE has order $p$ as well." :tutorial/id #uuid "380a28f7-f36f-50b0-982b-365f87b068bd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14656 :tutorial/title "Connecting the Definitions of the Order of Numerical Methods"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186222518 :tutorial/content "Let's use the smoothness property to find the Laplace transform of $\\mathcal L\\{t e^{-9t}\\}.$\n\nIf a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty)$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal{L}\\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s > a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\geq 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ t^n f(t) \\} = (-1)^n F^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\{ tf(t) \\} = -F'(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t) = e^{-9t}.$ Therefore, for $s > -9,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\dfrac{1}{s+9}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{s+9}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s+9)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s+9)^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{t{e}^{-9t}} & =-{F}^{'}(s) \\\\  & =-[-\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(s+9)^{2}},\\,s>-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5528e170-7114-565b-b165-7e6578eacf66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14686 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230201 :tutorial/content "Now, let's solve the separable differential equation for the velocity: $$v\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}r} = -\\dfrac{gR^2}{r^2}$$\n\nUsing separation of variables, we have $$\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{dv}{dr}\\,dr & =\\int (-\\frac{g{R}^{2}}{{r}^{2}})\\,dr \\\\ \\int v\\,dv & =-g{R}^{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{r}^{2}}\\,dr \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-g{R}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{r})+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{r}+C.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNote that $C=2C_1$ is a constant of integration.\n\nRecall that when the object is at the surface of Earth, $r=R.$ Let $v_0$ be the initial velocity of the object. Applying the initial condition $v(R) = v_0,$ we can solve for $C{:}$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}v(R)^{2} & =\\frac{2g{R}^{2}}{R}+C \\\\ {v}_{0}^{2} & =2gR+C \\\\ C & ={v}_{0}^{2}-2gR\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, the equation for the velocity is $$\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{v^2 = \\dfrac{2gR^2}{r} + v_0^2 - 2gR}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b874caf7-a7dd-505f-b799-e2877f2aed6c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14557 :tutorial/title "Modeling Velocity of an Object Projected Radially Outward"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230216 :tutorial/content "Consider the following boundary value problem (BVP).\n\n$$\ny'' + 9y = 0,  \\qquad y(0) = 0, \\quad y\\!\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0,\\quad 0\\leq x \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nFirst, we solve the differential equation to find the *fundamental solutions*, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x).$ \n\nSet $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\nm^2 + 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m = \\pm 3i,$ and thus the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\ny_1(x) = \\cos 3x \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad y_2(x) = \\sin 3x.\n$$\n\nThen, we construct the boundary condition matrix.\n\nThe BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$ if and only if the determinant of the boundary matrix $A$ is non-zero.\nNotice that our boundary conditions are in the form $\\alpha y + \\beta y' = 0$ with coefficients:\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot y(0) + 0 \\cdot y'(0) = 0, \\qquad\n1 \\cdot y\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) + 0 \\cdot y'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe define the matrix $A$ using these coefficients and our fundamental solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot {y}_{1}(0)+0\\cdot {y}_{1}^{'}(0) & 1\\cdot {y}_{2}(0)+0\\cdot {y}_{2}^{'}(0) \\\\ 1\\cdot {y}_{1}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+0\\cdot {y}_{1}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & 1\\cdot {y}_{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+0\\cdot {y}_{2}^{'}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}(0) & \\operatorname{sin}(0) \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = (1)(1) - (0)(0) = 1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\det(A)\\neq 0,$ we conclude that this BVP has the unique solution $y\\equiv 0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "96e6953a-3af4-5995-b093-def8a970f52b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14642 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]
